// The boss journal: Grubnak, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Revamp pages: Long Skewer (the blue shared line through his tank) and Dinner Bell (the Mains opener) are new; Grease Fire (the red rings round old grease)
// and Dinner Rush (the last plate is a platter for two) are rewritten, and Order Up! and Dinner Rush also carry Extreme's number signs.
// Extreme only (extreme: true): Kitchen Staff (the sous-chefs) and Flashover (the marked blast that lights grease).
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).grubnak = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Grubnak runs a kitchen like a bad parade ground: grease on the flagstones, fire in the corners, and a sergeant with two cleavers who never stands down. I lost my footing twice before I understood the room. Laced my boots tighter tonight. We go back at dawn.",
    gloomsworn: "The Cook. Fire along the floor, oil underfoot, cleavers for whoever stands in front. I have been struck by worse and kept my post. What I mind is the sliding; a dead man should at least be allowed to stand still. We go back in tomorrow.",
    thresher: "Grubnak keeps his kitchen like a host with a very long dinner: every course arriving at once and nobody permitted to leave. Greasy floor, hot stove, a cleaver for whoever stands in front. We were not ready for him yet, but there is time enough to learn the steps.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one hits like a side of beef falling off its hook, and never stops serving. Grease, fire, cleavers, all of it in my face. I am in love. Tomorrow I bleed for him properly, and he can keep the apron.",
    runeguard: "The Chef is legible once one knows his instruments; each cleaver, ladle and lid is written on him before it falls. Tonight I copied out only the first page, and misread half. Tomorrow I will have the whole text, and the margin notes besides.",
    gorgemaw: "At last, a kitchen that never stops sending out plates. My host's menu is long and his hand with the seasoning heavy, and the grease is a crime against honest fat. I sampled everything and lost my chair. Tomorrow I eat properly.",
    oathbroker: "I have read the Ogre Chef's terms and found them long: a continuous service, every course payable in full, no clause for rest. My co-signatories defaulted early, and so did I. Tomorrow the fine print is read before the first plate, not after.",
    ironpalm: "The cook speaks fast and never waits for an answer. Cleaver, ladle, lid, fire, and no pause between. We heard half of it tonight and answered less. I will find his silences tomorrow. Everyone has silences.",
    ranger: "Ogre in a long room. Greasy floor, casks along the walls for cover, fire in the corners. He never stops cooking, so there is no resting shot. Lost my footing, then my lane. Need to pace the place out before we go in again.",
    shadowblade: "My mark this time is an ogre in an apron, waving cleavers at whoever stands in front of him. Charming. He turns far too often for my taste, but he has a back like a barn door, and I found it. Twice.",
    pyromancer: "Grubnak cooks with fire, and badly! The whole kitchen smoked and spat, and I spent more time running from it than casting. An amateur with a stove. My fringe will never forgive him, and neither will I.",
    thermist: "Kitchen: hot. Ogre: hotter. Grease underfoot. I read it cold, clipped, sensible, and then everything came at once, flames and plates and cleavers, and I boiled over before I had written the first line. Overdone. Steadier tomorrow.",
    driftblade: "The cook never stops and neither did I, through the grease and round the casks and out past his cleavers, and it was glorious right up to the moment the floor caught fire under me. I have never been so happy to be hurt. Again tomorrow.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man is an ogre in an apron, and the stage is a kitchen: grease on the boards, fire in the wings, and the company missing half its cues. My Doll took it badly. (Exit, pursued by a cleaver.) The blocking will be redone by morning.",
    cleric: "The ogre feeds his kitchen with heroes, and he very nearly fed it us. Every course reached all of us, and the grease underfoot made the faithful careless. I said the long prayer over the cold stove. We will be wiser tomorrow, and in better shoes.",
    grovekeeper: "Hm. A kitchen is its own weather, dear: grease falls like rain, fire rolls in like a dry summer, and not a breath of still air. I'll learn its seasons. My lot came home singed but sprouting. That's a fair start.",
    chronomancer: "Grubnak serves in courses, each a little faster than the last, in the same order every time. I knew that already, of course, which is the trouble with knowing. Tonight we learned only the first bars of the tune.",
    alembist: "Ailment: one ogre with a kitchen, and a kitchen with opinions. Note for the shelf: he labels everything in advance and we did not. Collected a jar of the grease for study. It sparked. Two small fires, neither of them mine, I swear.",
    threadweaver: "That ogre ran us ragged round his greasy kitchen, and my little stitches wandered off to every corner of it. Ripped seams, frayed hems, and not one of them tied to anyone. Disgraceful needlework, mine. Tomorrow we hold the hem.",
    tidecaller: "Grubnak keeps his kitchen like a harbour in a gale, all spray and heat and not a breath of slack water. Every course reached all of us at once, and I kept the water low when it should have been high. Tomorrow I plan the tide around his courses.",
    lanternkeeper: "Grubnak's kitchen is all fire and grease, and I lit my lanterns where we were standing instead of where we would be. They were burned low before anyone reached them. Tomorrow I walk the round first and light ahead of the courses.",
    kenseier: "Grubnak shouts, but his swings keep an honest count. I mistimed most of them tonight and the kitchen floor took the rest of my pride. I will learn his beat before I learn his menu. Catch the beat.",
    houndmaster: "Big lad in a greasy kitchen, and Rust hates the smell. Fire in the corners, fat underfoot, cleavers for whoever stands in front. Lost both dogs to the floor before I learnt the room. Tomorrow we walk it first.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "Grubnak is down. Every course, every cleaver, and the line stood through all of it. I walked out of that kitchen on ruined boots and my own two feet. Post kept. The captain would have been proud.",
    gloomsworn: "The Cook is down, and all of us still stand. I stayed a while by his cold stove. He fed many to that fire. I was fed to a different one, long ago, and it was kinder.",
    thresher: "Grubnak came in at last, the whole long service of him, and went down on his own kitchen floor. Every course came due, and we were standing where we should be for each. His name was on the list, bless him, and I shall miss him a little.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one is down, and I have a scar from every course he served. Best meal I ever bled for. Shame it's over. Somebody tell the next one he has a standard to meet.",
    runeguard: "The Chef has fallen. Every sign came in its order, and every answer was ready a breath before it was needed. I have copied the whole text into my margins at last. As foreseen; though I will admit the third chapter surprised me.",
    gorgemaw: "My host is finished, and so is his menu: every course eaten, every dish sent back to him in pieces. I took his ladle as a keepsake. A long, heavy, mediocre meal, and I am sorry it has ended.",
    oathbroker: "The Ogre Chef's account is closed: every course paid in full by the undersigned, and his own balance collected at the last. I have filed the receipt. A second ledger lies open for the next creditor.",
    ironpalm: "The cook said everything he had to say. We answered every word. At the end he was quiet, and for the first time I could hear the kitchen breathing. I had no wish to speak into that silence. I bowed.",
    ranger: "Ogre's down. Whole service, every lane I could find, and not one wasted shot after the third course. Good hunt. Took a cask-stave as a trophy.",
    shadowblade: "The mark served his last course and toppled face first into it, glaring at our knight to the end. I lifted a silver spoon from his apron as I passed. A souvenir. It never saw me, even then.",
    pyromancer: "We beat Grubnak! I burned brighter than his whole stove, and for once the fire did what I told it. His kitchen is cold and mine is the only flame left in it. Tell the next cook I have opinions about his kitchen.",
    thermist: "Down. Kitchen cooling. I ran hot all night and somehow landed right in the middle every time. Readings steady, for once. Back to the middle, and stayed there. Marked the date on my glass.",
    driftblade: "We ran through every course he had and out the other side, and he dropped mid-swing, and I didn't land once all evening. The kitchen is quiet now. I ran one lap of it just to hear my own feet.",
    marionettist: "The curtain is down on the star, face first in the pudding. The company was magnificent; my Doll, unforgettable, and she left with the cleaver as a prop. A triumphant closing night. (Curtain. The Doll takes a bow, unasked.)",
    cleric: "The cook is gone, and every one of us walked out of his kitchen. I lit a candle by the cold stove for those who didn't, before us. He was hungry, in the end, like all of us.",
    grovekeeper: "The old ogre's season is over, dear. My lot weathered every course, and the kitchen is quiet now, like the morning after a storm. I left a pinch of sage by the cold stove. Something should grow there.",
    chronomancer: "Grubnak fell on the last bar, as he was always going to. I'd say I knew, but this time I truly didn't have to undo a single thing, and that is a different pleasure. I hummed his tune on the way out.",
    alembist: "Down at last. Patience, labels, and a party that stood where I told it did the work. Shelves nearly empty, one jar of his grease rescued, and one explosion, which was celebratory and therefore does not count.",
    threadweaver: "The ogre's down and not one thread snapped the whole service. My little stitches held from first course to last, and not a seam in the hem has gone. I would make him an apron, but he is too big.",
    tidecaller: "The cook is down, and his kitchen lies quiet as slack water. I raised the water for every course and let it ebb between them, and it was high each time he struck. Strange to miss the noise.",
    lanternkeeper: "The cook is down, and his kitchen is dark and cool at last. Every course had a light waiting where we stood: three lamps, four of us, and no wick burned to a stub. I left one burning by the cold stove.",
    kenseier: "The cook is down and the kitchen is quiet. Every cleaver had its beat, and by the end I was answering them without thinking. I cleaned the blade before the grease could set.",
    houndmaster: "He's down and the kitchen's quiet. Rust got the marrowbone, Ash got the scraps, and I got the stew. Good dogs. Had him by supper, near enough.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Grease Fire': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He raised his arms and the whole floor lit. Nothing for a shield to meet. Each old puddle of grease drew a red ring round itself, so I set the line beside the healer on clean ground, outside every ring, and we were mended before the flames came.",
      gloomsworn: "The oil lit under every one of us, and the grease we had left on the floor each drew a red ring of its own. I stood the watch by the healer on bare stone, clear of every ring, and took the mending before the fire. A ring is a grave with a view.",
      thresher: "Up went his arms, dear, and the whole floor caught, and every old puddle of grease wore a red ring like a little stage light. We gathered by the healer on clean boards, well outside the rings, and were mended before it burned. It burns out in its own time.",
      bloodreaver: "Fire across the floor, and every grease puddle flared in its own red ring on top of it! I longed to stand in one. Better to stay by the healer on clean ground, take the blaze, and be mended first, so the soft ones do not go down for it.",
      runeguard: "Arms raised: the whole floor lit, and each stain of grease drew its own red ring in the same breath, painted from the start of the cast. Stand beside the healer, on clean floor, outside every ring, and be mended before it falls. I read the rings first and the room second.",
      gorgemaw: "My host set the whole floor alight, and the greasy patches we had left flared in red rings of their own, like scorched garnish. We sat close to the healer on a clean tile, outside every ring, and were mended before the fire arrived. Rancid, and badly plated.",
      oathbroker: "A raising of the arms binds all parties at once, and by the rider every old patch of grease owes a red ring of its own. I stood at the healer's side on clean floor, outside each ring, our wounds made good in advance. A joint liability, honestly priced.",
      ironpalm: "His arms rose, and the fire spoke to the whole floor. Each old grease patch spoke too, in a red ring. We gathered by the healer on clean stone, outside every ring, and were whole before the fire came. Then we let it pass through us.",
      ranger: "Arms up, oil lit across the floor. Every old grease patch had a red ring round it, drawn from the start. Closed in on the healer, on clean ground clear of every ring, mended before it hit. No lane out of that one. Didn't need one.",
      shadowblade: "He raised his arms and the floor lit, and every old puddle grew a red ring to match, how thorough of him. I slipped in beside the healer on clean ground, outside all the rings, and had my scratches mended before the flames rose.",
      pyromancer: "Fire under everybody, and I resent being out-glowed. Every old grease puddle flared in its own red ring as well, so I picked clean floor beside the healer, well outside them, and stood the blaze out already mended. It was the colour of trouble.",
      thermist: "Arms up. Clipped: oil under everyone, a red ring round every old grease patch. Out of the rings, beside the healer, on clean floor. Then the heat hit and I flared, rushing, scalding, but we were together and mended before it. Too near a ring is too far.",
      driftblade: "He raised his arms and the oil lit under everyone, and every old grease puddle drew a red ring round itself, so I ran through the gaps between the rings to the healer and stood still on clean floor beside her, hating it, while the fire burned over and past us.",
      marionettist: "Cue: arms up, and the whole stage goes up with them. Every old grease patch carries its own red footlight, so the company gathered beside the healer on clean boards, outside every ring, mended in the wings beforehand. (The Doll, merely toasted, stood clear of a puddle.)",
      cleric: "He raised his arms and the floor caught alight, and each old patch of grease drew a red ring round itself besides. I drew everyone onto clean ground close beside me, outside the rings, and laid the light on them before the flames rose. Nobody fell.",
      grovekeeper: "Grease fire comes on like a dry summer, dear, and now every old puddle burns in its own red ring too, like scorched patches in a field. I gathered my lot on clean ground by the healer, outside every ring, and set the mending a breath ahead of it.",
      chronomancer: "Arms up, oil lit, and each old grease patch ringed in red well before the fire. I had the party on clean floor beside me, outside every ring, by the time it came. Mended before it landed, which is the only order I care for.",
      alembist: "Label: oil lit under the whole party, and a red ring round each patch of old grease. Dose: gather by me on clean floor outside every ring, mended beforehand; nothing cures it after. Keep the brandy away from the rings. Deliberately, this time.",
      threadweaver: "Grease under every stitch, and a red ring round every old puddle on top of it. I drew the party in around me on clean floor, outside every ring, and shared the burn out evenly, the way a good seam shares strain. Nobody frayed.",
      tidecaller: "The oil lit under the floor and every old puddle drew a red ring, like pools left by a spring tide. We gathered on dry stone close to the healer, clear of the rings, and I brought the water high before the flames came, so it was there when the fire broke.",
      lanternkeeper: "His arms went up and the oil took light, and each old puddle of grease drew a red ring round itself. I hung a lantern on clean floor beside the healer, outside the rings, and we crowded into its glow before the flames rose.",
      kenseier: "He raised his arms and the floor lit, with a red ring round every old grease patch. A blade has nothing to meet in a fire that comes up from the floor. We closed up on the healer, on clean ground clear of every ring, and the mending landed first.",
      houndmaster: "Whole floor lit, and every old grease patch had a red ring round it besides. No running from fire like that. Whistled Rust and Ash in on clean ground by the healer, well outside the rings, got mended before it caught, and sat it out.",
    } },
    'Mince': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One cleaver caught the light and that whole half of the kitchen was condemned. I kept his face on me while the line crossed to the dull half. Sometimes the heavy cleaver fell a heartbeat after, meant for me; I took it on the dull half, its circle clear of the line. A good drill.",
      gloomsworn: "One cleaver caught the light, and that half of the kitchen was spoken for. I kept his eyes on me while the others stepped over to the dull half. When the heavy cleaver came a heartbeat after, I took it there and walked its circle away from them. Nobody was buried today.",
      thresher: "One cleaver caught the light, and that half of the floor was the half due the blow. I took a stroll to the dull half before it fell. A heavier cleaver sometimes followed a heartbeat behind, for our knight, and I stayed well out of its circle. What a tidy step it is.",
      bloodreaver: "One cleaver glinted and that whole half of the room was about to get chopped. Tempting, very. But I crossed to the dull side like a good lad. Then the big cleaver, meant for me, came a heartbeat later: I took it there and dragged its circle off the others. Scars keep.",
      runeguard: "A glint along one cleaver is the heading of the page: that half of the floor is the chapter he means to strike. Cross to the other before it falls. The heavy cleaver, meant for me, sometimes follows a heartbeat behind; I took it on the safe half, its circle drawn well from the rest.",
      gorgemaw: "One flash of steel and he carved the room in halves, the lit half for the chop. Crude knife-work. I moved my chair to the dull half, and when the heavy cleaver came for me a heartbeat later, I ate it there and walked its circle away from my friends. Hacked, not cut.",
      oathbroker: "The glinting cleaver names the half of the room that pays; by the same clause the other half owes nothing at all. I moved accordingly. The heavy cleaver, due from me a heartbeat later, I received on the free half, leading its circle from my co-signatories.",
      ironpalm: "A cleaver glinted. It said: this side. I answered by standing on the other. Sometimes a heavier word, meant for me, followed a heartbeat later, and I took it on that quiet half, leading its circle away from the others.",
      ranger: "Watched which cleaver caught the light. That half got chopped. Crossed behind the line of it and waited, bow drawn. A heavy cleaver sometimes fell a heartbeat later on the knight; kept out of its circle. Not hard, once you know to look.",
      shadowblade: "One cleaver glittered at the mark's side, practically announcing itself. That half of the floor is his; the other half is mine. I strolled across and he chopped empty air. The heavy cleaver came down on our shield a heartbeat later, and I kept out of its circle.",
      pyromancer: "That cleaver glinted the colour of trouble, and I said so, loudly, a moment before the whole bright half of the kitchen was minced. I was already on the dull side, casting. The heavy cleaver fell on our knight a heartbeat later, and I kept clear of its circle.",
      thermist: "Which cleaver shines, which half dies. Cross. Then he chopped and I was already flaring back in from the safe side, rushing, burning. The heavy cleaver landed a heartbeat after, meant for the knight; I stayed out of its circle. The shiny half is too far, always.",
      driftblade: "One cleaver flashed and I was already round him and through to the other half, and the chop came down on empty stone behind me. The heavy cleaver followed a heartbeat later for our knight, so I stayed through and clear of its circle, and was back at his side before the dust had risen.",
      marionettist: "One cleaver flashes in the footlights, and that half of the stage is struck. The company exits to the other half on cue. A heavy cleaver may follow a heartbeat later for our lead, and the company stayed out of its circle. A clean change of scene, my Doll unscathed.",
      cleric: "One of his cleavers flashed, and everything on that side was chopped. Our archer lingered on the wrong half once, and I scolded gently afterwards. Cross to the dull half at the glint, dears, and when the heavy cleaver falls on our knight a heartbeat after, keep out of its circle.",
      grovekeeper: "Mince splits the kitchen like a plough splits a field, dear. The glinting cleaver marks the half that gets turned over. My lot crossed to the other furrow early and stayed dry, and kept out of the heavy cleaver's circle when it fell a heartbeat after, meant for our shield.",
      chronomancer: "The cleaver glints, a beat, then that whole half is gone. I had crossed to the dull side before the glint finished; I always will have. The heavy cleaver lands after that, for the knight, and I stayed out of its circle, naturally. The only promptness I am proud of.",
      alembist: "Label: the glinting cleaver. Meaning: that half of the kitchen is about to be chopped. Dose: stand in the other half. Second label: the heavy cleaver a heartbeat later, for our knight; stay out of its circle. I wrote both on the shelf in large letters, underlined in something that smoked.",
      threadweaver: "A cleaver winked on one side of that ogre, and he cut that half clean out of the pattern. I gathered my stitches on the other half before it fell, and kept them out of the heavy cleaver's circle when it followed a heartbeat after, meant for our knight.",
      tidecaller: "One cleaver flashed, and that half of the kitchen was as good as the shingle under a breaking wave. We crossed to the dull half while he was still raising it, and the knight held his gaze. When the heavy cleaver came a heartbeat after, for the knight, we stayed clear of its circle.",
      lanternkeeper: "One cleaver flashed, and that half of the floor was to be chopped. I picked my lantern up and set it down on the dull side, and the others followed the light across. The heavy cleaver sometimes fell a heartbeat after, meant for our knight, so I kept the lamps out of its circle.",
      kenseier: "A cleaver caught the light, and that half of the kitchen was his. I kept his face on me while the others crossed to the dull half. The heavy cleaver sometimes came a beat behind, meant for me; I met it there and led its circle off the rest. A glint is a warning.",
      houndmaster: "One cleaver caught the light, so that half was spoken for. Sent the dogs round to the dull side before I crossed myself. Heavy cleaver comes a heartbeat after sometimes, for the shield-man; kept the dogs out of its circle. Bright side's no place for a hound.",
    } },
    'Double Chop': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights he chops both halves, the glinting side first, the other right behind it. We stood on the dull half, then crossed into the ground he had just cut. Fresh-cut floor does not get cut twice.",
      gloomsworn: "He cuts both halves, the bright one first and the dull one after, when the nights are harder. We waited on the dull side, then crossed into what he had just cut. The only safe place in that kitchen is behind the blade.",
      thresher: "On the hard nights he cuts the floor twice, the glinting half first and the dull one after. I waited on the dull side, and the moment the first came down I hopped onto the half it had just struck. Ground already struck does not come due again, which is neat.",
      bloodreaver: "Both halves, one after the other! I waited on the dull side, and the instant the glinting half came down I walked straight into it. Clever, my friend. Not clever enough.",
      runeguard: "This one surprised me at first. The glinting half falls first and the dull half is struck behind it; wait on the dull side, then cross to the freshly cut ground. I added a second column to the page. The order is the answer.",
      gorgemaw: "Unsure of his portions, the cook chopped both halves of the table in turn. I sat the first out on the dull side and moved to the cleared plate for the second. Fussy, and wasteful with the knife.",
      oathbroker: "On the hard nights a second instalment follows the first: the glinting half is due, and then the other. We waited on the later account, then crossed to the settled one, for a creditor cannot collect twice from the same floor.",
      ironpalm: "He said this side, then that side. I stood where he would speak second, then moved to where he had already spoken. A word is never said twice in the same place.",
      ranger: "Two chops. Glinting half first, then the other. Waited on the dull side, then cut across into the first. Ground he's finished with stays quiet.",
      shadowblade: "He chopped one half, then the other, like a man who cannot decide. I waited on the dull side and slipped across into the cut half the moment it fell. Indecision is so unbecoming.",
      pyromancer: "Both halves, one after the other! I stood on the dull side, grumbling, then dashed into the glinting half the instant it was done, mid-cast. Two chops, both of them wasted on the floor where I had been.",
      thermist: "Glint side, then the other side behind it. Wait on the other. Then hot: first chop lands, I cross over, burning, as the second slams down behind me. A hair's breadth.",
      driftblade: "He chops one half and then the other, so I waited on the dull side, hating it, then ran through into the cut ground the moment it fell, and the second chop landed in my wake.",
      marionettist: "A two-scene number: the glinting half is chopped, then the other. The company waits in the second, then crosses into the first on the downbeat. (The Doll, crossing on cue, to applause.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights the cleaver falls twice: the glinting half, then the other. Wait on the dull side, then step into the ground that was cut first. I watched every friend manage it, and was quietly proud.",
      grovekeeper: "Double Chop is a field turned twice, dear. The glinting furrow first, the other after. My lot waited in the second and stepped into the first once it was turned, like rotating crops.",
      chronomancer: "Glinting half, then the echo on the other. I waited out the first beat on the dull side and was in the cut half for the second. A canon in two voices. Obvious in hindsight, which is the only sight I have.",
      alembist: "Two doses, one after another: the glinting half, then the other. Wait in the half that doesn't glint, then cross into the one that just fell. Order matters, as with reagents, and I have the eyebrows to prove it.",
      threadweaver: "Glinting half first, the other right behind it. I kept my stitches on the dull side, then drew them across into the cut half all together, no strays.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights he swung twice, the bright half and then the dull. We waited on the dull half, then walked across onto the ground he had just cut, like walking the sand a wave has only just left.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights he swung twice, the bright half and then the dull. We stood on the dull side while the first blow fell, then crossed onto the cut ground, and I carried my lantern over only once it had landed. The light moved when we did.",
      kenseier: "The bright half first, then the dull half right behind it. The others waited on the dull half and crossed into the ground he had just cut. Two strokes, and I admired how the second followed the first.",
      houndmaster: "Bright half first, then the dull half right behind it. We stood on the dull half and stepped over into the turf he'd just chopped. Dogs first, me after. Chopped ground stays quiet.",
    } },
    'Ladle Spin': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He whirled the ladle round himself, scalding anything close. I stepped off him with the line, out past the edge, and came straight back in under his chin once it stopped, for the lid sometimes followed hard behind, its red already on the floor. Gave ground on purpose.",
      gloomsworn: "He swung the ladle about himself and scalded all who stood close. I walked away, the others with me, and came straight back in under his chin the moment it stopped, because the lid sometimes followed hard behind. Out, then in. I have done both in my time.",
      thresher: "Grubnak whirled his ladle and scalded everything near him, so I stepped back past the edge of it, humming. The moment the steam stopped I strolled straight back in under his chin, dear, for the lid sometimes comes hard behind, its red already on the floor.",
      bloodreaver: "Soup flew in a ring round his ladle, and I backed off for once. Hated it! Then straight back in under his chin, because the lid sometimes dropped right behind it, red already on the floor. Scalded boots and all.",
      runeguard: "The ladle swings low and soup churns about his feet: a ring is drawn before the spin. Step outside it, then return the instant it turns; the lid sometimes follows hard behind, its red drawn on the floor from the start. The ring is the inscription.",
      gorgemaw: "Soup, served by flinging it round in a circle. I declined, stepped back past the splash, and came straight back for the next course, since the lid sometimes follows hard on its heels, red already on the floor. Thin, and much too hot.",
      oathbroker: "The ladle binds only those who stand close, and the obligation lapses the moment the spin ends. I stepped beyond its terms, then returned at once under his chin, for the lid is sometimes due hard after, its red drawn from the start.",
      ironpalm: "The ladle said: step away. I stepped away. When it had finished speaking, I came back at once, under his chin, for the lid sometimes answers hard behind it. Its red was on the floor already.",
      ranger: "Ladle out, ring of soup round its feet. Backed off to the edge, then straight back in under its chin as the spin ended. The lid sometimes dropped hard behind, red already on the floor. A boar chasing its own tail.",
      shadowblade: "A tidy circle of soup, as if his back needed defending. I stepped out of reach, then came straight back in under his chin, for the lid sometimes follows hard behind, its red already on the floor. No time for manners, on either side.",
      pyromancer: "A ring of scalding soup round his feet, so I stepped well outside it and kept my spells coming from there. The moment it stopped I hurried straight back in under his chin, since the lid sometimes follows hard behind, red already on the floor. Soup, I maintain, is no medium for a fire.",
      thermist: "Ladle low, soup churning. Out of the ring. Then I swung right back in under his chin, hot again, before the steam had cleared. The lid sometimes follows hard behind, red already on the floor. Too eager. Right.",
      driftblade: "He spun the ladle, and I slid out past the edge of the soup, a gust round a candle, and back through the steam and in under his chin the instant it stopped, because the lid sometimes comes hard behind, its red on the floor from the start.",
      marionettist: "The ladle number: a ring of hot soup at centre stage, and the company exits upstage until the spin ends. Then straight back downstage under his chin, for the lid sometimes follows on the heels, its red chalked on the boards from the start. (The Doll, barely in the wings.)",
      cleric: "When he swings the ladle, everything near him is scalded. Our knight and the other close fighters stepped back out of the ring, then straight back in under his chin, for the lid sometimes follows hard behind and its red is already on the floor. I did not have to mend a single burn.",
      grovekeeper: "The ladle spin is a little whirlwind at his feet. Easy weather: step out past the churned ground, then straight back in under his chin, dear, for the lid sometimes comes on its heels, its red already on the floor. My lot learned it quickly, and no one steamed.",
      chronomancer: "The ladle comes round once: out on the wind-up, and back in on the very next beat, under his chin, because the lid sometimes follows hard behind it, red already on the floor. A short, dull little tune with a quick second verse.",
      alembist: "Scalding soup in a ring round him. Wait outside it until the ladle stops, then straight back in under his chin, for the lid sometimes follows hard behind, red already on the floor. Soup, unlike brandy, does not explode. A pity.",
      threadweaver: "The ladle came round and my stitches stepped out of the soup all at once, nice and even, then came straight back in together under his chin, for the lid sometimes follows hard behind, red already on the floor. A pleasure when people move like one seam.",
      tidecaller: "He turned the ladle about himself and the ring of soup scalded all who stood near. Those at his feet walked out past the edge, I with them, and when it ebbed we came straight back in under his chin, for the lid sometimes follows on its heels, its red already on the floor.",
      lanternkeeper: "He turned the ladle about himself and the ring of soup scalded all who stood close. Those at his feet stepped out past its edge, my lantern on our knight, then straight back in under his chin the moment it stopped, for the lid sometimes follows hard, its red already drawn.",
      kenseier: "He spun the ladle about himself and scalded the ground near him. We stepped out past its edge together and came straight back under his chin on the beat it stilled, for the lid sometimes follows hard behind, its red already on the floor. A retreat is a stroke too, if it is timed.",
      houndmaster: "He spun the ladle and scalded everything near him. Walked out past its edge with the dogs and came straight back in under his chin the moment it stilled, as the lid sometimes drops hard behind, red already on the floor. Don't dawdle, lads.",
    } },
    'Lid Slam': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lifted the lid overhead and everything away from him took the slam. I stood under his chin and the line closed in behind me. Under him is the only safe ground in the room. Odd, but correct.",
      gloomsworn: "The lid went up, and everything away from him took the slam. The only shelter was under his chin, so I stood there and the others pressed in behind. The dark taught me to stand where the fear is.",
      thresher: "When the lid went up, the whole kitchen away from him came due at once, all but the ground beneath his chin. So I tucked in right against him, close as the last step of a waltz, and let it fall on empty floor.",
      bloodreaver: "The lid went up and the shock rolled out over the whole room, except right at his belly. So in we came, nose to apron with my friend. Cosy.",
      runeguard: "The lid held aloft throws a shadow outward, and the quiet lies beneath it. Come in under him before it falls; the far ground is where the sentence lands. I trust the inscription over my own instincts here.",
      gorgemaw: "He lifted the lid on his dish and smacked the whole room with it, except those seated right at his elbow. I always sit close to the kitchen; one learns where the food is. Underwhelming.",
      oathbroker: "By an exemption nobody reads, those within arm's reach of the lid owe nothing to the slam, while everyone at a distance pays in full. I closed in with a clear conscience and a firm grip on my pen.",
      ironpalm: "The lid rose. It said: come close. We came close. It struck everything else, and we stood in the quiet it made.",
      ranger: "Lid up. Everywhere hit but right under it. Gave up my range and walked in. Ugly. Worked.",
      shadowblade: "The lid rose over the mark's head and the whole room beyond him was doomed. So I came in close, right behind him, where I'd rather be anyway. Safest seat in the house.",
      pyromancer: "Everywhere away from him gets flattened, so I had to run in beside the ogre, who smells awful, and then out again to burn. I have decided to be brave about it.",
      thermist: "Lid overhead. Clipped: the quiet middle is under him. Close in. Then the slam rippled out past us and I was hot and cutting before it reached the walls. Closer than I like.",
      driftblade: "The lid rose and the safe place was in close, so I went straight in under it, nearly through his apron strings, and the slam rolled out through the room behind me.",
      marionettist: "Blocking for the lid: downstage, into his very shadow, where the slam takes the empty house. Everyone else is a corpse in the cheap seats. The Doll sat under his elbow, quite safe, and slightly smug.",
      cleric: "When he raised the lid, the safe place was right up against him, which frightened some of us. I walked in first so they would follow, and the slam passed over our heads. Courage is mostly company.",
      grovekeeper: "The lid slam is like hail: it falls everywhere but under the old tree. My lot crowded in at his feet before it fell. Funny shelter, that ogre, but a shelter is a shelter.",
      chronomancer: "Lid up, everyone in, and then the slam rolls out past us. The middle is quiet every time. I was standing there before I decided to.",
      alembist: "Everything away from him slammed flat, so stand close underneath. Counterintuitive. I labelled it twice, in different inks.",
      threadweaver: "The lid went up and I drew every thread in tight to his boots before it fell. Anyone left slack at the edges would have been flattened.",
      tidecaller: "The lid went up and the whole room away from him was to be flattened. Odd to learn it, but the calm was at the eye of the storm: we stood right beneath his chin and let it break around us. A squall has a still middle.",
      lanternkeeper: "The lid went up, and the safe place was right under his chin, which is a hard place to walk to. I set a lantern there first, so there was a warm spot to walk toward. We stood in the glow and the slam passed over us.",
      kenseier: "The lid went up and the far floor took the slam. Under his chin was the one quiet place, so I stood there and the others pressed in behind me. I like a blow whose safe place sits closest to its owner.",
      houndmaster: "Lid goes up and the far floor takes the slam. Only dry spot is right under his chin, so in we went, dogs behind me. Like standing under a bull's belly. Ugly, but it works.",
    } },
    'Tenderize': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A red ring settled on me and followed me about while he wound up, then locked, and his ghost cleaver came down on it edge first. I braced in the last instant and took it whole; the others kept clear of my ring.",
      gloomsworn: "The ring ran after me until it locked, and the cleaver fell out of the air onto it, edge first. I drew the dark in and took it whole; there was nowhere to go and no reason to. The rest kept out of the ring.",
      thresher: "A red ring crept after our shield and set, and then the ghost of a cleaver came down on it, edge first and neat as a signature. The knight cannot step aside from that one, so he takes it, bless him, and the rest of us keep well out of his circle.",
      bloodreaver: "Tenderize! The ring stuck to me like a hound, locked, and down came his ghost cleaver, edge first, right through me. A good one; I took it full and drank it back, and the others kept out of my circle.",
      runeguard: "A red ring followed me until it locked, and then the spectral cleaver fell upon it, edge first. It cannot be outrun; it can be met, with a ward laid a breath before, while the rest keep outside the ring.",
      gorgemaw: "The signature dish, dropped from above onto my plate: his ghost cleaver, edge first, on the ring that trailed me. I could not step out of it, so I opened wide and swallowed it whole while my friends kept off my side of the table. Meaty.",
      oathbroker: "The circle trailed me until it locked, and the spectral cleaver fell upon it, edge first; the principal cannot evade, and any bystander within the circle is liable for a large share. I accepted delivery in full, and my co-signatories stayed outside the ring.",
      ironpalm: "The ring followed me, then stopped. The cleaver came down out of the air, edge first; I could not step aside, so I did not try. I raised my hands at the last instant and took it, and the others stood outside my circle.",
      ranger: "Red ring on the knight, trailing him till it set. Ghost cleaver dropped on it, edge first, and he took it where he stood. Stayed well clear of his circle.",
      shadowblade: "A red ring followed our shield about, then settled, and a ghost cleaver fell out of nowhere onto it, which is rather obvious of him. I stay well out of other people's circles.",
      pyromancer: "A red ring chased our knight around like a puppy, and then his ghost cleaver dropped on it, edge first, huge as a door! The knight takes it, our healers lit him up beforehand, and I stayed out of that circle, naturally. Quite a show, from a safe distance.",
      thermist: "Cold: ring on the shield, trailing, then locked. Cleaver drops, edge first, nothing for him to dodge; everyone else, out of his circle. I ran hot with worry, but the healers had him topped, and it passed.",
      driftblade: "The ring slid after our shield and then locked, and the ghost cleaver dropped on it, and I went round the edge of it, through the gaps and never in his circle, while the knight took it full because there was no running from that one.",
      marionettist: "His great moment: a red spotlight trailing the leading player until it sets, then the ghost cleaver drops from the flies, edge first. The knight takes the whole blow centre stage; the rest of the company keeps out of his light. (The Doll, upstage, admiring.)",
      cleric: "The red ring followed our knight about, and when it set, the ghost cleaver fell upon him, edge first. He cannot step aside, so I lay the light on him before it lands, not after, and the rest kept out of his circle.",
      grovekeeper: "It came like hail on one tree: a red ring that trails our shield and then sets, and the ghost cleaver dropping on it. I planted my mending on him before it fell, and my lot stayed out of his circle.",
      chronomancer: "The ring follows the knight, locks, and the cleaver arrives a beat later, edge first, onto where he already is. There's nothing to dodge, so I had mended him before it landed, and the rest stood out of his circle. Sensible, not clever.",
      alembist: "One ghost cleaver, edge first, onto the ring that tracks our shield. Dose him before it lands, not after, and keep everyone else out of his circle. Do not stand beside the patient.",
      threadweaver: "The ring trailed our knight till it set, and the cleaver fell on it, edge first. I had tied his thread to mine already, so a little of the weight ran down the seam to me, and everyone else kept out of his circle. The seam took it.",
      tidecaller: "A red ring settled on the knight and trailed him until it set, and the ghost cleaver fell on it. He could not step aside, so I had the water high around him before it landed, and the rest of us kept out of his ring.",
      lanternkeeper: "A red ring settled on our knight and followed him until it set. I hung a lantern on him and kept the wick high, and the rest of us stayed out of his circle. The glow was already there when the ghost cleaver fell.",
      kenseier: "The ring followed me until it locked, and a cleaver fell out of the air onto it. I met it with the blade just as it landed, and the others kept clear of my ring.",
      houndmaster: "A ring ran after the shield-man, and a cleaver fell out of the sky onto it. Sent the lads well clear of his circle till it came down. Not yours, not your patch.",
    } },
    'Long Skewer': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A blue line ran out through me this time, not the red one. A shared blow: the whole line stepped onto it close behind me and we took it together. I stood at the front and took the larger share. Alone, I would have carried it all.",
      gloomsworn: "The line ran blue, through me, and the dark took the larger share without complaint. The others stepped onto it behind me and the blow came shared. Alone on it I would have taken all of it, and I have taken worse.",
      thresher: "A blue line this time, dear, not red, and the skewer flew down it. Everyone stands on it, close behind the knight, so the blow is shared, the knight bearing the larger part. Nobody sits it out; those left off it catch a smaller piece anyway.",
      bloodreaver: "Blue line, straight through me! Not mine alone, though: the others piled onto it behind me and we split it, the heavy half on me, which suited me fine. A fool who stands off the line still catches some. I was glad of the company.",
      runeguard: "The line was drawn blue this time, through me: a blow to be shared. All of us stood upon it, close behind me, and I bore the larger part. Alone I would have borne it all; left off the line, each takes a little anyway.",
      gorgemaw: "A blue skewer this time, to be shared like a platter. Everyone crowded onto the line behind me and I took the biggest bite, as is proper. Left alone, I would have eaten all of it, with pleasure. Stragglers were still served a little. Bland.",
      oathbroker: "A blue line, so a joint obligation: all parties stand upon it behind me and divide the sum, my share being twice theirs. Were I alone upon it, I would bear the whole; were nobody on it, each would pay a small part regardless.",
      ironpalm: "The line came blue, through me. It asked to be answered together. We stood on it, close behind me, and I took the larger share. Left alone, I would have answered all of it. Left off the line, everyone is touched a little.",
      ranger: "Blue line from it through the knight, trailing till it set. Shared hit. Stood on the line, close behind him; he takes the heavy part. Off the line, you take a little anyway. Stood in the crowd. Worked.",
      shadowblade: "A blue line ran out through our knight, which for once meant walking toward the danger. We all stood on it, close behind him, and shared the blow, his portion the larger. Left off it, everyone catches a little. Better company than I like.",
      pyromancer: "This skewer glowed blue, not red! Blue is shared, so I hurried onto the line behind the knight with everyone else, and he took the larger share, bless him. Off the line, you catch a little all the same. I hate a crowd, but I hate being hit alone more.",
      thermist: "Line from him through the shield, blue, trailing, then locked. Clipped: the skewer is shared. Get onto the line, close behind the knight. He weighs double. Then the hit came, warm and thinned out, and nobody went down. Off the line, you still catch some.",
      driftblade: "The line went blue and trailed our knight and I ran in through the others to stand on it, close behind him, hating that it meant standing still, because the skewer is shared and he takes the bigger half. Anyone off it catches a little as well.",
      marionettist: "A blue beam this time, trailing our leading player until it sets, and the skewer flies down it. An ensemble number: the whole company stands on the beam behind him and shares the line, his part the larger. (The Doll, behind the knight, beaming.)",
      cleric: "The line ran blue through our knight and followed him until it set. I gathered everyone onto it, close behind him, so the blow was shared, his portion the larger. Left alone he would have borne it all. I laid the light on him before it flew.",
      grovekeeper: "A blue line this time, dear, trailing the knight until it sets, then the skewer flies down it like hail on one row. My lot stood in the row behind him, close together, and shared it. He bears the larger part. I planted my mending on him first.",
      chronomancer: "The line goes blue through the knight, trails, sets, and the skewer follows. Everyone stands on it behind him before it flies. He takes the larger share; alone he would take all. I had mended him already, will have, did.",
      alembist: "One skewer, labelled blue: a shared dose. Everyone onto the line, close behind the knight; he takes the larger measure. Alone on it, he takes the lot; nobody on it, and each of us took a small measure anyway. I stood in the line, flasks held to one side.",
      threadweaver: "The line ran blue from him through our knight, trailing till it set. I drew every stitch onto it, close behind him, so the blow was shared along the thread, the knight bearing the heavier end. A stitch left off the line still got tugged.",
      tidecaller: "The line ran blue through our knight, and blue means shared. We waded onto it behind him, close together, and the weight divided between us like a swell over many rocks, the knight taking the larger share. I had the water high round him first.",
      lanternkeeper: "A blue line ran out through our knight and followed him until it set. I counted us onto it: three lights close behind him, one on him, so the blow was shared and his share the larger. I hung the lantern on him first. Nobody stood off the line.",
      kenseier: "The line came blue, through me, and the skewer flew down it. A shared blow: the others stood on it behind me, and I took the larger share. A blade cannot cut a share in two, so I did not try. Alone on it I would have taken everything.",
      houndmaster: "Blue line through the shield-man, trailing till it set. Shared one, so everyone onto it close behind him, dogs too, and he takes the bigger half. Nobody left off it, or you all catch some regardless. Called Rust in tight. Sit.",
    } },
    'Meat Toss': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Grubnak hung a slab over each of the others, every one wanting its own patch of floor. They dropped theirs at the edges, apart, away from the line. Footing kept. The stain thins in time, shrinking and dimming, and is gone altogether if fire reaches it.",
      gloomsworn: "Slabs hung over the others, each wanting a place of its own, each leaving grease where it landed. They went to the edges and apart. The stain thins with time, and fire eats it at once. I remember where each fell.",
      thresher: "A slab hung over my head, darkening, so I carried it off to the wall, well away from the others, and let it drop where nobody stands. The patch fades in its own hour, shrinking and dimming. Fire takes it at once, so do mind where it lies.",
      bloodreaver: "He hung steaks over the others, never me, insulting. They each took a corner and left the grease out by the walls, not under my feet. Clean floor to bleed on. The stains shrink and dim after a while, or burn off if fire finds them.",
      runeguard: "A steak on a chain over each of us save the one before him; the grease it leaves is a mark that will outlast the page. Walk apart to the edge and let it fall there. It dims and shrinks with time, and fire ends it at once.",
      gorgemaw: "Steaks dangled over my friends but not me, which I took personally. They carried their plates apart, to the walls, so the grease would not pool under the table. The stains shrink and dim in the end, and fire clears them faster.",
      oathbroker: "Meat Toss: each co-signatory but the shield receives a separate account, payable apart, with a lien of grease left where it was settled. We settled out by the walls. The lien is not perpetual: it shrinks and dims, and fire discharges it at once.",
      ironpalm: "A steak hung over each of the others. Each walked alone to the edge, and the grease stayed there, where nobody needed to stand. The stain faded in the end, shrinking and dimming. Fire would have finished it sooner.",
      ranger: "Steak over my head. Walked to the wall, clear of the others, dropped it there. Puddle stays out of the lanes. Remember where. It shrinks and dims in time; fire clears it at once.",
      shadowblade: "A steak dangled over my head, terribly forward. I carried it off to a quiet corner and left my little grease puddle where nobody walks. Tidy. It shrinks and dims in time, a shame; fire would take it faster still.",
      pyromancer: "A steak hung over my head like a prize! I carried it to the wall and dropped my grease there, because grease and I are a bad combination. It shrinks and dims after a while, or burns off if fire reaches it. I would have liked to help it.",
      thermist: "Steak over me. Walk to the edge, apart. Drop it. The grease stays, and fire will burn it, so out of the way is the only way. The stain fades in time, dimming and shrinking first. Everything cools eventually.",
      driftblade: "A steak hung over me, so I carried it round through the gaps between the others to the far wall and dropped it there, out of every path I'd want to run later. The stain shrinks and dims in time, and fire clears it faster.",
      marionettist: "A steak lowered over each player but the lead. Each exits to the wings, apart from the others, and drops the grease offstage. The stains shrink and dim with time, or burn away if fire reaches them. (The Doll, carefully not underneath anything.)",
      cleric: "The steaks hang over everyone but our knight. Each of us went to our own place by the wall, so no two puddles touched, and the grease stayed where nobody had to walk. It dims and shrinks in time; fire takes it sooner.",
      grovekeeper: "Meat Toss sows grease, dear. Each of my lot carried theirs to the edge of the field and planted it there, apart, where it wouldn't spoil the paths. It lingers, shrinking and dimming; fire clears it at once.",
      chronomancer: "Steaks over all but the knight; each walks to their own spot by the wall and drops it. The grease stays, shrinking and dimming a little with every beat, and fire clears it at once. I chose my spot last time; I chose it again.",
      alembist: "Steaks over all but our shield, each leaving grease and then a stain that waits for fire. Each to their own spot by the wall. Grease is a reagent; I labelled where it went. It shrinks and dims in time, and fire ends it at once.",
      threadweaver: "Steaks over my stitches. I loosened every thread so each could walk to the wall alone and leave their grease out of the way. The stain shrinks and dims in time, or burns away if fire reaches it. Then back together.",
      tidecaller: "A slab hung over each of us but the knight, and every one wanted a patch of floor to itself. I walked to the wall with mine and let it fall well apart from the others. The stain ebbs and thins in time, and fire takes it at once.",
      lanternkeeper: "A slab hung over each of us but the knight, and each wanted its own patch of floor. I took mine to the wall alone, and left a lantern in the middle of the room for us to gather at after. The grease thins in time; fire takes it sooner.",
      kenseier: "Slabs hung over the others, each wanting floor of its own. They walked to the edges and dropped them well apart. The stain thins with time, and fire clears it at once. I kept the cook looking at me.",
      houndmaster: "Slab hung over my head. Walked it out to the wall, clear of the rest, and let it drop there. Kept Rust back or he'd have rolled in it. Fire clears the stain quick enough.",
    } },
    'Family Meal': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A silver cloche came down over one of ours. One plate for the whole table. The line gathered under it and shared the blow, and I joined them the moment I could leave my post. Split, it was light work.",
      gloomsworn: "A silver cloche came down over one of our own, and one plate does not feed a table. We went under it together and split the blow, and I stood in the ring too. Split, it is a small thing for each.",
      thresher: "A silver cloche came down over one of our number, and a single portion for the whole table is lighter when shared. So we all walked in under it, like guests squeezing onto one bench, and the weight went light.",
      bloodreaver: "Family Meal! One big plate, split between us all. We crowded in under the cloche, shoulder to shoulder, and I made sure to be there for my share. It hurts less shared.",
      runeguard: "A silver cloche lowered over one of us is a clause that names the whole company. All gather beneath it and divide the blow; the fewer present, the heavier each share. I checked my arithmetic twice.",
      gorgemaw: "A platter for the whole table, under a silver lid! My friends gathered beneath the cloche and I joined them, and we shared it out. I would have eaten it all alone, frankly. Rich, but shared.",
      oathbroker: "A joint liability, under a silver cloche. We all gathered beneath it and divided the sum between us; any who absent themselves raise the share of the rest.",
      ironpalm: "The cloche fell over one of us. It asked for all of us. We gathered and answered together, and the weight was small.",
      ranger: "Cloche over one of us. Everyone into it. Shared the hit. Hate crowding. Necessary.",
      shadowblade: "A cloche descended over a friend, and the whole company had to crowd in beneath it to share the portion. Not my usual company, but I squeezed in. One must dine with someone.",
      pyromancer: "Everybody crowded under the silver cloche to share the blow, which is a very cosy way to be hit. This time the heat was shared out evenly, the only sharing I enjoy.",
      thermist: "Cloche over one of us. Gather in, close, closer. The blow landed split between all of us and I barely warmed. Together is a lovely temperature.",
      driftblade: "The cloche came down over one of us and we all had to stand still under it together, which I despise, so I circled until the last breath, slipped in through the gap and took my share.",
      marionettist: "An ensemble number: a silver cloche over one member of the company, and everyone onstage beneath it, sharing the line. No soloists tonight. (The Doll, front and centre, naturally.)",
      cleric: "Family Meal is served to one, and the rest of us must come to the table, or that one takes it all. I gathered everyone in under the cloche and we shared it. Grace before meals, as my mother said.",
      grovekeeper: "Family Meal is a harvest for the whole table, dear. My lot gathered close under the cloche and split it between us, roots together. Nobody went hungry, nobody fell.",
      chronomancer: "The cloche goes down over one; on the beat everyone is under it with them, and the blow is split. I was there first. I usually am. It is the only advantage of the job.",
      alembist: "One heavy portion, served to one. Dilution works on most things, so everyone went under the cloche and it divided between us. Note: it works on soup as well. I checked on the way out.",
      threadweaver: "A cloche came down over one of my stitches, and I drew every thread tight to bring them all in under it. Shared among us all, it barely stung.",
      tidecaller: "A silver cloche came down on one of us, and one body cannot carry that blow alone. We all came in beneath it and shared it, many hands on one rope, and the water I had raised carried the rest. Split, it was a ripple.",
      lanternkeeper: "A cloche came down on one of us, and one body cannot carry that blow alone. We all came in beneath it, with a lantern set in the middle so the glow covered every one of us. Four of us, one light, and the blow was split.",
      kenseier: "A silver cloche came down on one of ours, one plate for the whole table. We all went under it and split the blow, and I left my place to join them when I could. Between us it was no more than a tap.",
      houndmaster: "Big silver lid came down on one of us, one plate for the whole table. Called the dogs in and we all squeezed under it. Shared, it's a rap on the knuckles.",
    } },
    'Larder Toss': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He hurled casks at the two of ours standing farthest off. They stepped out from under before it landed, left the casks on open floor, and kept going toward the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind. Solid cover. We kept them for the next blast, and I counted them like rations.",
      gloomsworn: "Casks came flying at the two of us standing furthest out. We sidestepped at the last moment, left them lying in the open, and kept running toward the far walls, since the pot boils over close behind. Keep them away from the Cook, or he gathers them in.",
      thresher: "He flung casks at the two of us farthest out. Whoever's it was led it onto open ground, stepped off at the last, left it standing, and kept running to the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind. Not near Grubnak, dear, or he catches it.",
      bloodreaver: "Barrels, thrown at whoever stood furthest off! They dodged; pity, a barrel to the face is honest work. The casks stayed where they fell, worth keeping, and we ran for the far walls right after, because the pot boiled over close behind.",
      runeguard: "The shelf is torn loose and a circle trails the two farthest of us. Carry it somewhere useful, step aside before it lands, and keep going toward the far walls, away from him; the pot boils over close behind. The cask is shelter for what follows. Written in the margin: not near him.",
      gorgemaw: "He flung his own larder at the two guests farthest from the table. They let the casks land beside them, not on them, and ran on for the far walls, because the pot boils over hard behind. Poor service. Good barrels.",
      oathbroker: "Delivery to the two co-signatories farthest from the Chef. They signed for it at a sensible address, stood aside on arrival, and ran on toward the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind. The cask left standing is collateral against the roar.",
      ironpalm: "He threw a cask to the farthest of us. They stepped aside, and kept walking to the far walls, away from him, as the pot would speak close behind. The cask stayed. Something to stand behind, later.",
      ranger: "Cask came at me for standing furthest out. Led it to open floor, stepped off at the last, ran on for the far walls; pot boils over close behind. It stays. Good cover. Not too near the ogre, or he catches it.",
      shadowblade: "A cask thrown at me for keeping my distance, which is hardly fair. I placed it neatly on open ground, stepped aside, and kept running for the far walls, away from him, since the pot boils over close behind. A lady needs something to hide behind.",
      pyromancer: "He threw a cask at me! Me! I led it somewhere sensible, out of the fire, hopped clear, and kept running for the far walls, because the pot boils over close behind. Then a lovely barrel to hide behind for the roar.",
      thermist: "I'm farthest, so a cask is mine. Lead it to clear floor, out of the fire, not too near him. Leave it be and go. Keep running to the far walls: the pot boils over close behind. It sat there, solid, and I knew exactly what was coming. Steadier already.",
      driftblade: "A cask came for me because I had drifted farthest out, and I was glad of the company: I swung it round to open floor, slipped from under at the last breath, and ran on through toward the far walls, away from him, for the pot boils over close behind, leaving it standing.",
      marionettist: "Props flung from the larder at the two players farthest upstage. They step aside on cue, leave the scenery standing, and keep running to the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind; it matters in the next scene. My Doll admired the set dressing.",
      cleric: "The two who stand farthest from him have a cask thrown at them. They led it to open floor, stepped out before it fell, and ran on to the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind. I blessed those barrels later. Not near the cook, mind.",
      grovekeeper: "Larder Toss plants casks in the field. Whoever's farthest out leads them to good ground, steps aside before they fall, and keeps going toward the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind like a squall. Cover is planted like a hedge, dear.",
      chronomancer: "Casks at the two farthest, just as the shelf comes loose. Step aside before it lands, leave it where it's useful, and keep running for the far walls; the pot boils over close behind. The roar comes after. It always comes after.",
      alembist: "Casks thrown at the two of us farthest off. Step out from under, land them somewhere useful, and keep running for the far walls, away from him, since the boil-over follows close behind. Note for the shelf: casks are shelter. Keep them, and do not sit on the full ones.",
      threadweaver: "The two stitches farthest out got a cask each. I let their threads out so they could lead the casks to clear floor and step aside, and then keep running toward the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind. Slack, used on purpose, is no sin.",
      tidecaller: "Casks came for the two of us standing farthest out. We led them onto open floor, stepped aside at the last, and ran on toward the far walls, for the pot boils over close behind like a rising swell. A cask is a rock to shelter behind. Never near the cook.",
      lanternkeeper: "Casks came for the two of us standing farthest out. I kept my lanterns with the party while the pair led theirs onto open floor, stepped aside, and ran on toward the far walls, since the pot boils over close behind. Leave them lying, never near the cook.",
      kenseier: "Casks flew at the two standing farthest out. They stepped aside at the last moment, left them on open floor, and kept running to the far walls, for the pot boiled over a beat behind. A cask is a wall that arrives on its own. We kept them away from the cook.",
      houndmaster: "Casks come for the two stood furthest out. Led mine onto open floor and stepped off at the last, then ran on for the far walls, away from the big lad, since the pot boils over close behind. Cask just lies there. Not near him, he gathers them up.",
    } },
    'Gut Blast': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He roared across the whole kitchen. No shield blocks that. I put a cask between myself and Grubnak like the rest of the line and kept still behind it.",
      gloomsworn: "He roared through the whole kitchen, and only something solid turns that. A cask went between me and him, and the others crouched behind their own.",
      thresher: "His belly swelled and I knew that look, the deep breath before a bellow. It reaches the whole kitchen and only something solid stops it, so I tucked in behind a cask, or the pot later, with him on the far side. Quite cosy.",
      bloodreaver: "He bellowed so hard it shook the walls. Even I hid behind a barrel for that one; it is a scar with nothing in it. Cask between us, then back to work.",
      runeguard: "His belly swells and the breath is drawn deep; the blast leaves a shadow behind anything solid. A cask between oneself and him, or the pot later, and the sentence cannot reach. I told the others where to kneel.",
      gorgemaw: "Revolting manners: the ogre belched at the entire dining room. I ducked behind a cask with my friends, him on the far side, and let the stench pass.",
      oathbroker: "Everyone not sheltered owes the roar. The clause exempts any party behind a cask, or later the pot, with the Chef on the far side. We found shelter, and I filed a note on the quality of the casks.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed in. I stepped behind a cask. He shouted. It went past me, as wind goes past a stone.",
      ranger: "Roar covers the whole kitchen. Only cover is a cask, or the pot later. Put it between me and the ogre. No line for once, only a wall.",
      shadowblade: "When the ogre drew breath to roar, I tucked myself behind a cask with it squarely between us. A roar cannot touch what it cannot find.",
      pyromancer: "The loudest thing I have ever heard, and I once blew up a library! Hide behind a cask, or the pot later, with Grubnak on the far side. My ears are still applauding.",
      thermist: "Find a cask. Put it between me and him. Low. Then the roar came and its heat went round me on both sides and never touched me. Too close, but not too far.",
      driftblade: "His roar fills the whole room, so the only answer is a cask, and I swung in behind one with him on the far side and stayed perfectly still while it howled round me.",
      marionettist: "His great aria: a roar that fills the whole house. The company takes cover behind the scenery, the casks, with the star on the far side. My Doll hid behind a barrel and mouthed along.",
      cleric: "When he draws in that great breath, everyone must hide behind a cask with him on the far side. Anyone caught in the open is hurt terribly. I steered our people to the barrels and counted heads twice, as one does in the small hours.",
      grovekeeper: "Gut Blast blows like a gale off the moor, dear. My lot sheltered in the lee of the casks, or the pot when there was one, with him upwind. Every windbreak counts.",
      chronomancer: "Belly swells, a long held beat, then the roar. Cask between you and him before the beat ends. I learned that the hard way once. Once was enough, and I made sure of it.",
      alembist: "One roar, whole kitchen, and the only remedy is a cask, or the pot after the fire, between you and him. Note: each blast cracks the casks, and on the harder nights a cask shelters only two.",
      threadweaver: "He drew breath and I pulled every one of my stitches behind a cask, him on the far side. Anyone left hanging out in the open would have heard from me afterwards.",
      tidecaller: "He drew a great breath for a roar that would cross the whole kitchen, and only something solid turned it. I crouched in the lee of a cask with the ogre on its far side, as a gull does behind a rock in a gale.",
      lanternkeeper: "He drew breath for a roar that would cross the whole kitchen, and only something solid would turn it. I set a lantern behind a cask, on the side away from him, and we crowded into its warm lee.",
      kenseier: "He drew a great breath and roared across the kitchen. There is nothing to cross a blade with in a roar, so I put a cask between us and crouched, as the others did. Even a quiet man hides from the loud ones.",
      houndmaster: "He roared across the whole kitchen. Can't outrun a noise, but you can sit behind a barrel. Put a cask between us, dogs under my arm, and waited it out.",
    } },
    "Butcher's Charge": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lowered his head at me and charged till he hit something. I put bare brick behind me, stepped aside at the last, and he hit the wall and stood dazed. The line got off his path, then scattered at once as the marks came down, minding each puddle. Not the stove, not the casks.",
      gloomsworn: "He lowered his head at me and ran. I had bare brick at my back, waited, and slid aside at the last breath. He met the wall and stood dazed. The others kept off his road, then scattered at once as the marks came down, each minding their puddle.",
      thresher: "He put his head down at our knight and charged. I kept off that line, and when the knight led him into the brick and he stood dazed, the marks came down right after: I scattered at once, dear, and minded where my puddle fell. A tidy bit of timing, that.",
      bloodreaver: "He lowered his head and came for me, my friend! I led him at bare brick and stepped aside, and he hit it like a drunk. Stuck and dazed, and the marks dropped over our heads right on the landing, so we scattered first and I carved after, puddles away from the stove.",
      runeguard: "His head lowers toward me and a path is drawn to the far wall. Aim it at brick, never the stove or our casks, and step aside once it sets. The marks fall over our heads right after he lands: off the line first, then apart at once, mindful where each puddle goes.",
      gorgemaw: "Headfirst at me, like a bull at a gate. I stood with bare brick at my back and stepped aside, and he ate the wall instead. Dazed, and the marks came straight after: off his line, then apart at once, each puddle laid well from the stove. A fitting course for him.",
      oathbroker: "The Chef runs on until met by something solid, and the terms do not say what. I nominated bare brick, neither the stove nor our casks, and stepped aside. A second clause follows on his landing: off his line first, then apart at once, each mindful where the puddle falls. Dazed, he defaulted.",
      ironpalm: "He lowered his head at me. I gave him a wall to speak to, then stepped aside. A second voice followed at once: marks over our heads. We left his road, then each went apart, mindful where the puddle fell. He listened to the wall for a while.",
      ranger: "It lowers its head at the knight and runs until it hits something. Keep off the line. Brick stops it dazed. Marks dropped on us right after the landing, so we fanned apart at once, puddles clear of the stove. Good time to shoot.",
      shadowblade: "Our knight led the brute head-down into a brick wall. Delightful. The rest of us kept off the line, then parted at once, for the marks came down right after, puddles well clear of the stove. While he sat there dazed I took my time with his back.",
      pyromancer: "He charged our knight, who aimed him at bare brick! Not the stove, not the casks. Everyone kept off his line, then scattered the moment the marks came down, puddles mindful of the stove. Then he was stuck there seeing stars, and I poured everything into him.",
      thermist: "His line runs to the knight. Off it. Brick behind the knight. Then he hit the wall and the marks came down over us at once. Apart, quick, puddle away from the stove. Then hot, all over him while he swayed. Overheated, delighted.",
      driftblade: "Head down and straight as a thrown spear, and bare brick at the end of it, so I cut through off his line and watched him meet the wall, then ran apart from the others the moment the marks came down, my puddle clear of the stove. Then I circled him while he saw stars.",
      marionettist: "The charge: the star runs at the lead player, who steps aside and lets him meet the back wall of the set. Not the stove, not the scenery. On the landing the marks drop at once: the company clears his line, then scatters, each puddle well placed. (The Doll, applauding the stunt work.)",
      cleric: "He lowers his head at our knight and runs until he hits something. Our knight learned to lead him into bare brick, where he stands dazed. The rest of us kept off his path, then scattered at once, since the marks came down right after, and minded where each puddle fell.",
      grovekeeper: "The charge is a bull through a fence, dear. Our shield turned him toward bare brick, not the stove or the casks, and my lot stepped off his path, then scattered at once as the marks came down, puddles clear of the stove. He stood dazed after, like a struck ox.",
      chronomancer: "Head down, the path follows the knight, and then, in the instant before he goes, it stops following. Aim at brick, step aside on that instant, and scatter on the landing, for the marks come hard behind it, puddles clear of the stove. He was dazed, as I had known he would be.",
      alembist: "One ogre in a straight line until he hits something. Aim him at bare brick, off our line, never the stove or our casks, then scatter at once, for the marks follow the landing. Mind where the puddle goes. The stove erupts; I wrote that one twice.",
      threadweaver: "He charges our knight until he hits something. I pulled my stitches off his line, and our knight brought him round to a bare wall, not the casks, not the stove. Then the marks came down on the landing, and I let the threads out at once, mindful where each puddle fell.",
      tidecaller: "He lowered his head at the knight and ran until he met something. Our knight led him to bare brick, away from the stove and the casks, and slid aside at the last. The rest of us left the road, then drew apart at once when the marks came down, each mindful of the puddle.",
      lanternkeeper: "He ran at our knight with his head down. The knight led him to bare brick, clear of the stove and the casks, while the rest stayed off the road. The marks came down on the landing, so I set my lantern apart from the others at once, and minded where the puddle went.",
      kenseier: "He lowered his head at me and ran. I stood with bare brick behind me and stepped through his road at the last breath, and the cook met the wall and stood dazed. The others kept off the stove and the casks, then scattered the beat the marks came down.",
      houndmaster: "Head down, and he ran for the shield-man. Both dogs stayed off his road and off the stove. He hit the brick and stood dazed, then the marks dropped over us straight away, so we split up at once and I watched where the puddle went.",
    } },
    'Dinner Bell': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The pass bell rang and the room took a blow with it. Light, and nothing to block. I kept the line near the healer, took it standing, and had my footing set before the first course. A bell means orders are coming.",
      gloomsworn: "A bell rang, and the room rang with it, a light blow for everyone. No shield for it. We stood near the healer and took it. After the bell, the real work. I have heard worse tolls, and answered them.",
      thresher: "A little silver bell, dear, and the whole room felt it, though only lightly. Nothing to step round; we stood close to the healer and let it pass. It announces the main course, rather like a footman clearing his throat.",
      bloodreaver: "He rang the bell and the room shook, a tap, hardly a scar. Shameful. I stood by the healer with the rest and took it, and saved my appetite for whatever the bell was calling in.",
      runeguard: "The bell rings, the stove line glows, and a light blow reaches everyone. There is nothing to dodge; stand near the healer and take it, then read the next course as it arrives. The bell is only the title page.",
      gorgemaw: "The pass bell: my host announcing the main course with a clang that rattled every seat. A light blow, and nowhere to hide. I stayed near the healer and took it as an appetiser. Cheap showmanship.",
      oathbroker: "The bell constitutes notice of the main course, and a light charge falls on all parties at once; no clause allows evasion. We stood together at the healer's side and paid in common. The real obligations follow the bell.",
      ironpalm: "A bell rang. The kitchen said: begin. The blow was small and reached everyone, and there was nowhere to go. We stood close to the healer and took it, and then the first course arrived.",
      ranger: "Bell on the pass, stove line lit. Light blow across the whole room, nothing to dodge. Stood by the healer, took it. The next course came right behind it.",
      shadowblade: "A bell, and a light blow for every one of us, which seemed unnecessary. No shadow to hide in, so I stood by the healer and took it with my dignity intact. The main course always follows a bell.",
      pyromancer: "He rang the bell and the stove line blazed up and the whole room took a little thump! Nothing to dodge, nothing to burn. I stood near the healer, took it, and was ready for the main course before the echo faded.",
      thermist: "Bell. Burners glowing all down the pass. A light blow for everyone, no way round. Stand close to the healer, take it, stay level. Then the heat of the main course, and I was ready for it.",
      driftblade: "A bell rang and the burners flared down the pass and a light blow rolled through the room, nothing to run through, so I stood near the healer, annoyingly still, and took it, and was off the moment the first course began.",
      marionettist: "Cue: the pass bell. The burners light along the wings, and a light blow reaches every seat in the house. No blocking for it; the company stood by the healer and took it. The main course comes on directly after. (The Doll curtsied, uninjured.)",
      cleric: "The bell rang for the main course, and a light blow reached us all. I kept everyone near me and laid the light on them beforehand, so it was no more than a bruise. The bell is a warning; I heard it as one.",
      grovekeeper: "The bell is the first drop before a storm, dear: a light shower over the whole room, nowhere to shelter. My lot stood by the healer and took it. The main course comes on its heels like proper weather.",
      chronomancer: "The bell rings, the burners catch down the line, and a light blow lands on everyone, always on the first stroke of the clapper. Stand close to the healer and take it. The main course is the next verse.",
      alembist: "Label: pass bell. A light dose for the whole room, and no remedy but standing close to the healer for it. After the ring the main course begins, so I topped up the shelf in the gap, one bottle fewer than I wished.",
      threadweaver: "The bell rang and the room took a light blow, thin and even. No snagging was needed; we stayed close to the healer and let it run through us. The main course was already coming, so I kept my needle threaded.",
      tidecaller: "The bell rang and a light swell crossed the room, reaching every one of us, with no wall to stand behind. We stood near the healer and took it on a high tide. After the bell, a fuller sea.",
      lanternkeeper: "The bell rang, and the burners along the pass lit up. A light blow reached all of us, with no corner to hide in. I walked in beside the others and we took it close to the healer. A bell means a long night ahead.",
      kenseier: "A bell, and a light blow with no beat to meet. The blade cannot turn a bell. We stood near the healer and took it, and by the last ring I was ready for the first course. A courteous way to open a service.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad rang the bell and the burners flared. Light knock for everyone, nothing to run from. Held Rust and Ash in by the healer and sat it out. The bell means the main courses are coming, so we were ready.",
    } },
    'Chop Chop': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One cleaver glinted, and the ladle or lid on his back said what came after. We crossed to the dull half, then went out for the ladle or in for the lid. Two orders, one drill.",
      gloomsworn: "A glint on one cleaver, and a ladle or lid on his back to follow. We moved to the dull side first, then out for the ladle or in for the lid. I read both before taking a step.",
      thresher: "A glint on one cleaver and a tool on his back behind it: two appointments, and I like to know both before I attend either. Dull half first, then out for the ladle or in for the lid.",
      bloodreaver: "Chop, then whatever's on his back! Glinting half first, so I took the other side. Then the lid meant in, the ladle meant out. Two lessons in one breath, my friend.",
      runeguard: "He speaks twice: the glinting cleaver names the half, and the tool on his back names what follows. The dull half first, then in for the lid or out for the ladle. A two-line verse, and I had it by heart.",
      gorgemaw: "A two-course dish: first the minced half, then whatever hung on his back, ladle or lid. I sat on the dull side, then took the right seat for the second. Hurried, but edible.",
      oathbroker: "A compound clause: the glinting half is due first, and the tool on his back states the terms of the second, ladle without, lid within. I read both before signing either.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke twice. The glint: this half. The tool: near, or far. I answered both.",
      ranger: "Glinting side gets chopped. Then the tool on its back: ladle, out; lid, in. Read both before moving.",
      shadowblade: "Two announcements at once, how showy: the glinting cleaver for the half, the tool on his back for what follows. I took the dull half, then followed the tool.",
      pyromancer: "Two dishes at once! The glinting cleaver chops a half, then the tool on his back follows: ladle, get out; lid, get in. I managed both and only dropped one spell.",
      thermist: "Glint, half; back, tool. Dull side first. Then hot: the ladle meant out, so out, and then straight back in, burning. The rhythm of it made me giddy.",
      driftblade: "The glint tells you which half and the tool on his back tells you what next, so I slipped through to the dull half and out of the ladle's ring and back in through the steam.",
      marionettist: "A scene with a quick change. The glinting cleaver strikes one half of the stage, then the tool on his back sets the next cue, ladle out, lid in. (The Doll, hitting both marks.)",
      cleric: "Chop Chop asks two things together. The glinting side is chopped, then the tool on his back follows: the ladle wants us out, the lid wants us in. I watched each friend get it right, and was prouder than I should be.",
      grovekeeper: "Chop Chop is a frost and then a thaw, dear. The glinting half falls first, then the tool on his back tells the weather: ladle, keep out; lid, come in. My lot read both before moving.",
      chronomancer: "Chop, then the tool a beat behind. The glint picks the half, his back picks in or out. A two-bar phrase. I have heard it so often now that I hum it.",
      alembist: "Two labels at once. First: the glinting cleaver, that half chopped. Second: the tool on his back, ladle out, lid in. Read both labels before pouring. I once skipped one, and the ceiling still has the mark.",
      threadweaver: "The glinting half first, then the tool on his back: out for the ladle, in for the lid. I moved my stitches as one piece, twice, and dropped none.",
      tidecaller: "A glint on one cleaver, and a ladle or a lid on his back to follow. We crossed to the dull half first, and then went out for the ladle or in for the lid. Two swells, one behind the other, and neither hard alone.",
      lanternkeeper: "A glint on one cleaver, and a ladle or a lid on his back to follow. I carried my lantern to the dull half first, and then out for the ladle or in for the lid. Two moves, and the light kept ahead of us for both.",
      kenseier: "A glint on one cleaver, and a ladle or a lid on his back to follow. We took the dull half first, then went out for the ladle or in for the lid. Two beats, and the second is the one that catches people.",
      houndmaster: "A glint on one cleaver, then a ladle or a lid on his back. Dull half first, then out for the ladle or in for the lid. Two things to read, so I read them twice.",
    } },
    'Order Up!': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One plate each, laid round the kitchen and served in turn. I stood on mine like a sentry on a painted square until it was dished. On the hardest nights a number hung over each of us and each plate carried one; the wrong man on it spoiled it for all.",
      gloomsworn: "Plates were laid round the kitchen, one for each of us, served in turn. I stood on mine and waited. On the worst nights a number hung over each of our heads, and the plates carried numbers too; the wrong hand on one spoiled it for everyone. The dead wait well.",
      thresher: "Plates went down all round the kitchen, one apiece, the fullest rim served first. I stood on my own and waited to be seated. On the hardest nights each of us wore a number over our head, and each plate bore one: take only your own, dear, or the plate spoils for all.",
      bloodreaver: "A plate for each of us, served in turn round the room, and I stood on mine until the rim ran full. On the cruellest nights a number floated over every head and every plate wore one. The wrong hand on a plate spoiled it for everybody. Take your own.",
      runeguard: "Cloches round the kitchen, their rims filling at different rates; the fullest is served next, working round from the top left. Each claims one plate. On the hardest nights a number rides over each head and on each plate; only that hand may serve it, or it spoils for all.",
      gorgemaw: "Plates for every guest, laid round the room, and served in turn. I stood on mine until it was dished. On the hardest nights each guest wore a number over his head, and each plate was marked to match: the wrong guest at a plate spoils the dish for everyone. Take your own.",
      oathbroker: "One plate per co-signatory, served in sequence round the room, the fullest rim next. On the hardest nights each party carries a number, and each plate is marked with one; delivery to the wrong hand voids the dish for all. Each signs for his own.",
      ironpalm: "Plates around the room, one for each of us. Each stood on their own until it was served. On the hardest nights a number hung over every head, and each plate bore one. Only the matching hand answers it. A wrong hand spoils it for all.",
      ranger: "One plate each, round from the top left, fullest rim first. Stood on mine. Hardest nights put a number over each head and one on each plate. Take the matching one. Wrong hand spoils it for everyone.",
      shadowblade: "Plates laid round the kitchen, one per guest. On the hardest nights each of us carried a number over our head and each plate wore one, and a plate taken by the wrong guest was spoiled for everybody. I stood on mine until it was served, naturally.",
      pyromancer: "Plates! A ring of them, served round from the top left, fullest first. On the very worst nights a number hangs over each of us and over each plate, and the wrong person on one spoils it for everyone. Mine was easy to find. I stood on it and cast.",
      thermist: "Plates, one each. Know yours before it starts. Fullest rim lands first. On the hardest nights a number floats over each head and each plate; wrong hand, spoiled for everyone. Then the rush: ran to mine, stood still till it went, ran off. No plate left cold.",
      driftblade: "A plate each, all round the kitchen, served in turn, and I stood on mine and waited, the worst thing a person can ask of me. On the hardest nights a number rode over each head and each plate, and a wrong hand on one ruined it for everybody, so I ran to mine.",
      marionettist: "Every player has a plate, a mark, and a cue: served in sequence, the fullest rim next. On the hardest nights each player wears a number over the head, and each plate is cast to match; a wrong player on one spoils the scene for all. My Doll stood on hers, beaming.",
      cleric: "One plate set before each of us, and the room served in turn. I walked among them making sure each friend stood on theirs. On the hardest nights a number hung over every head and every plate, and a wrong hand on a plate spoiled it for all. We learned to look up.",
      grovekeeper: "The order rail is a row of seedlings coming up one at a time, dear. Each of my lot tended their own, fullest rim first. On the hardest nights each head wears a number and each seedling wears the same; a wrong hand on one blights it for everybody.",
      chronomancer: "Plates in a ring, each rim filling toward its own beat, served round from the top left. On the hardest nights a number floats over each of us and each plate, and only the matching hand may stand on it. I knew mine, as I have known it before.",
      alembist: "One plate per guest, served in order round from the top left, fullest rim first. On the hardest nights each patient wears a number and each plate carries one; take only your own, because the wrong hand on a plate spoils it for everyone. Assign them beforehand.",
      threadweaver: "A plate for each stitch, served round the room in turn. On the hardest nights a number hangs over every head and sits on each plate, and only the matching stitch may stand on it, or the plate spoils. I let the threads out so everyone could reach their own.",
      tidecaller: "A plate for each of us, set out like stones across a ford, served one after another, the fullest rim first. I stood on mine until it was dished. On the hardest nights a number hung over each head and each plate, and only the matching hand might take it, or it spoiled for everyone.",
      lanternkeeper: "A plate was set down for each of us, and I counted them against our heads. On the hardest nights each of us had a number overhead and each plate carried one, so I lit the right plate for the right person. A wrong hand spoiled it for all.",
      kenseier: "A plate for each of us, served round the kitchen in turn. I stood on mine until it was dished. On the hardest nights a number hung over each head and over each plate, and only the matching hand may take it; a wrong hand spoils it for all. Nothing here wanted a blade.",
      houndmaster: "A plate each, served round the room in turn. Stood on mine till it was dished. Hardest nights, there is a number over every head and one on every plate, and the wrong hand spoils the plate for all. Counted heads against plates twice, since the dogs won't.",
    } },
    'Rolling Meatballs': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lobbed meatballs against the walls. Each wobbled with its lane drawn across the room, then rolled straight. I kept my footing out of every lane until the last had passed. Ground between lanes is good ground.",
      gloomsworn: "Meatballs wobbled at the walls and rolled straight, each lane drawn across the floor. I stood between the lanes and let them pass. It is the one fair thing in that kitchen: it tells you where death will roll.",
      thresher: "Meatballs rocked against the walls, each with its path drawn straight out ahead of it. I stood between two paths and watched them trundle by. A tidy kitchen, for such a messy cook.",
      bloodreaver: "Giant meatballs, rolling across the room! I'd love to be hit by one, but it knocks you sideways and that's undignified. Out of their lanes until they pass.",
      runeguard: "Each meatball lands at the wall and wobbles, its lane drawn across the kitchen before it rolls. Stand clear of every lane until all have passed. It is nearly polite of him to underline his own sentences.",
      gorgemaw: "Meatballs rolled across the dining room instead of being served. I would have eaten them had they held still. Stood between the lanes and watched them go by. Overcooked, and impolite.",
      oathbroker: "Each meatball is a debt in transit along a fixed route, drawn before it moves. I stood outside every route and paid nothing, which is the finest sum.",
      ironpalm: "Round things rolled toward us. Each showed its path first. I stood where none would go, and let them speak to the wall.",
      ranger: "Meatballs wobble at the wall, then roll straight across. Lanes drawn. Stood between them, a few paces off the nearest. Easiest shooting of the night.",
      shadowblade: "Meatballs, bowled at us one by one. Each sits at the wall and wobbles before it rolls, showing its path. I stood between the paths and watched them go by. Very rude, and very predictable.",
      pyromancer: "Enormous meatballs rolling straight across the kitchen, and each one wobbles first to show its lane. I stood clear and burned on from there. Gravy on my boots, again.",
      thermist: "Meatballs at the walls, wobbling, lanes drawn. Stand between. Then they rolled, hot and fast, and I did not move a hair until the last one passed. Pulse high, hands cold.",
      driftblade: "The meatballs wobble at the walls and then roll straight through the room, so I wove between the lanes and through behind each one as it passed, and never got flattened once.",
      marionettist: "Enormous meatballs rolled across the stage, each along a lane marked in advance. The company stands between the lanes and does not improvise. The Doll was narrowly missed. Gasps from the house.",
      cleric: "The great meatballs wobble against the walls before they roll across the room. One knocked our archer clean off the plate, and I mended the poor thing and scolded gently. Do keep out of the lanes.",
      grovekeeper: "Rolling Meatballs come down their lanes like a spring flood down the furrows, dear. Read where each one wobbles and stand between the channels. My lot stayed dry.",
      chronomancer: "They land, they wobble, a beat, then they roll, one after another. Stand between the lanes until the last has passed. I had counted them before the first landed.",
      alembist: "Meatballs, large, rolling. Stand outside every lane; each wobbles first and shows its path. Note: when they come with the plates, the plates sit between the lanes. Kept the leftovers for study.",
      threadweaver: "Meatballs wobble at the wall, then roll straight across. I let my stitches out between their lanes and gathered them back once the lanes had gone quiet. Easy needlework, with a bit of nerve.",
      tidecaller: "They wobbled against the walls and then rolled straight across, each lane drawn on the floor. I stood between the lanes and let them pass like long waves along a quay. A little patience, and the floor was mine again.",
      lanternkeeper: "They wobbled against the walls and then rolled straight across, each lane drawn on the floor. I set my lantern between two lanes and stood beside it, and the others came to the light and out of the way.",
      kenseier: "Meatballs wobbled against the walls, then rolled straight along lanes drawn on the floor. I stood between the lanes and let them pass. A blow that announces its road is a courteous one.",
      houndmaster: "Meatballs wobble at the wall, then roll the lane straight across. Stood between the lanes and held the dogs back, for a hound will chase anything that rolls.",
    } },
    'Flare-Up': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Cook heaved his pot onto the stove and the top of the kitchen caught for good. We were off that ground before it lit, mended against the heat. A smaller parade ground after, but I only need enough floor to stand on.",
      gloomsworn: "He heaved the stockpot on the stove and the top of the kitchen caught, for good. We were off that end before it lit, and braced for the heat. A smaller room, but all of us in it.",
      thresher: "He heaved the stockpot onto the stove and the top of the kitchen went up in flame, and there it stays. I had strolled down out of that end before it caught. A smaller kitchen after, but room enough to dance.",
      bloodreaver: "He set his own stove alight, the whole top of the room! The heat reached everyone, a good warm scar. Then I stayed out of the fire for the rest of the night. Burn once, learn.",
      runeguard: "The stockpot is lifted toward the stove. Abandon the top of the kitchen before it catches, for it burns from then on, and have everyone whole for the heat that follows. I drew a line across the floor plan.",
      gorgemaw: "The cook scorched his own kitchen and left us standing in the smoke. Out of the top of the room before it caught, then. The heat seasoned everyone. Badly burnt, and all of it his fault.",
      oathbroker: "The top of the kitchen is foreclosed for the remainder of the service, and the heat is owed by all. I vacated before it caught, and counted it prudent.",
      ironpalm: "He set the stove alight. The top of the room burned. We had left before it spoke. The heat reached us anyway, as a shout reaches the next room.",
      ranger: "Pot onto the stove. Top of the room burns, for good. Out of it before it catches; paced the new edge afterwards. Less floor. Fewer lanes.",
      shadowblade: "Halfway through, the mark heaved his pot onto the stove and set the top of the kitchen ablaze. I had already left that end of the room. Fire is so terribly common.",
      pyromancer: "He set his own kitchen on fire! Very good fire, too, the colour of real trouble. Out of the top of the room before it catches; it never goes out. I was jealous, and I went anyway.",
      thermist: "Fire all along the top of the room, roaring, and I ran so hot I nearly forgot to move. Cold now: leave the top before it catches. It never goes out.",
      driftblade: "He heaved the pot onto the stove and the whole top of the room went up, so I ran down and away before it caught, and now the room is small and I resent every wall of it.",
      marionettist: "The interval: the ogre sets the upper stage alight for the rest of the run. The company moves downstage before it catches, and the heat reaches the stalls anyway. (The Doll, smoking slightly.)",
      cleric: "When he lifts the pot onto the stove, everyone must leave the top of the kitchen; it burns from then on. The heat reaches all of us anyway, so I had everyone mended first. A little warmth is no sin.",
      grovekeeper: "Flare-Up is the summer wildfire, dear. The top of the kitchen burns from then on, and its heat reaches us all. I moved my lot down the slope before it caught and planted before the heat.",
      chronomancer: "Pot on the stove, a beat, then fire across the top for the rest of the fight, and a wave of heat. We had left the top by then. We were all very punctual.",
      alembist: "The top of the kitchen is ablaze for good, and the heat reaches everyone. Out before it catches, everyone mended first. Note: smaller workshop now, and I miss the shelves at that end.",
      threadweaver: "The whole top of the kitchen alight, and it never went out. I drew my stitches down out of it before it caught, and we took the heat close together. Hems burn first, so keep them tucked.",
      tidecaller: "He set the pot on the stove and the top of the kitchen burned for good. We came down out of it before it caught, and I kept the water high against the heat that reached us anyway. A smaller room, but the sea is still the sea.",
      lanternkeeper: "He put the pot on the stove and the top of the kitchen burned for good. We came down out of it before it caught. The heat reaches everyone anyway, so I kept the wicks topped up and let the lanterns burst as it came.",
      kenseier: "He set the pot on the stove and the top of the kitchen caught for good. We came down off that ground before it lit, with the mending already laid. A smaller room, but a room.",
      houndmaster: "Pot on the stove, and the top of the room burns for good. Walked down off it before it caught, dogs with me. Smaller yard, still a yard.",
    } },
    'Flambé': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Brandy laid in stripes and lit one after another. I stood where the fire would come last, then took the ground behind the flame. Old grease I kept my boots off; boots are the one kit I cannot requisition.",
      gloomsworn: "Brandy ran across the floor in stripes and the fire took them one by one. I stood where it would come last, and when it had passed I walked onto the ashes. The old grease I would not touch; it remembers.",
      thresher: "He laid brandy in stripes and lit them one after another. I waited in a stripe that would go late, then stepped onto the one already burnt out, where there was nothing left to catch. The old grease I kept clear of, naturally.",
      bloodreaver: "Stripes of brandy, catching one after another! I waited, then stepped in behind the flames onto ground already burnt. Grease stains in a stripe go up too. Kept my boots off them.",
      runeguard: "Brandy in stripes, a blue flame running along each before it catches. Step into the stripe that has just burnt, behind the fire, and keep clear of old grease. A fire reads from one end to the other like anything else.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole floor flambéed, stripe by stripe. Pretentious. I stepped into each stripe once it had burnt and kept off the old grease. Too much brandy, too little steak.",
      oathbroker: "The stripes come due in sequence, and a stripe already paid owes nothing further. I moved onto settled ground, clear of the grease liens, which remember their debts.",
      ironpalm: "Brandy on the floor. The stripes caught, one by one. I stood where the fire had already spoken, and it had nothing more to say.",
      ranger: "Stripes of brandy. They catch in turn. Step into the one already burnt. Keep off grease.",
      shadowblade: "Brandy splashed in stripes and lit in turn. I waited for one to burn, then stepped into it, behind the fire. The grease stains catch too; I avoided them, naturally.",
      pyromancer: "Flambé! Stripes of brandy going up one after another! Absolutely beautiful. Step into the stripe that's just burnt and keep off any old grease in it. I wanted to stay and admire, but admiring is how one gets cooked.",
      thermist: "Brandy, stripes, flame all down the room, and I was burning just watching. Cold: wait. Step into the one that just burnt. Off the grease stains. Steady now.",
      driftblade: "The stripes catch one after another, so I ran through behind the fire, into each burnt stripe as it went and round the grease stains, setting foot nowhere that hadn't burnt yet.",
      marionettist: "A pyrotechnic interlude: brandy across the stage in stripes, lit in sequence. The company steps into the ones already burnt and keeps off the grease. (The Doll, flame-resistant. Mostly.)",
      cleric: "He splashes brandy across the floor in stripes, and they catch one after another. Wait, then step onto ground that has already burnt; any grease in a stripe goes up with it. A kinder fire than most.",
      grovekeeper: "Flambé burns like stubble fires down a field, row by row, dear. Wait, then walk onto the burnt row behind the flames. The old grease we sowed earlier burns too, so keep off it.",
      chronomancer: "The stripes catch in order, a beat apart. I step into each the moment after it's burnt, a tune I was humming before it started. Old grease catches when its stripe does.",
      alembist: "Brandy in stripes, catching in turn, and every grease stain in them lights. Step into a stripe that has already burnt. Note: this is exactly how flambé is meant to work, and I am furious he does it better.",
      threadweaver: "Stripes of brandy caught one by one, so I pulled my stitches into the burnt stripes behind the flames, well away from the old grease. The cleanest pass of the night.",
      tidecaller: "Brandy lay across the floor in stripes and caught one after another. I waited, then stepped onto the stripe that had already burnt, and kept clear of the old grease, which goes up with it. Burnt ground is a beach after the wave.",
      lanternkeeper: "Stripes of brandy caught one after another. I waited for one to burn out, then carried my lantern across onto the black of it and the rest came after. Old grease in a stripe goes up with it, so keep off that.",
      kenseier: "Brandy lay in stripes and lit one after another. I waited for the flame to pass, then stepped onto the stripe that had already burnt, and kept off the old grease. Burnt ground does not burn twice.",
      houndmaster: "Brandy laid in stripes, lit one after the other. Waited for the flame to pass and stepped onto cold ash. Ash, off the old grease. Sit.",
    } },
    'Stockpot': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the fire he dragged the stockpot down to the middle. It is solid. It stops a charge and shelters from his roar. Marked it as ground to work from. A fixed point is worth any number of good intentions.",
      gloomsworn: "When the fire was done he dragged the pot to the middle of the room. It does not move. It stops his charge and keeps his roar off us, so I fought near it. A thing that will not move is a comfort.",
      thresher: "After the fire he dragged the stockpot down to the middle and left it, good and solid. It turns his charge and keeps his roar off us, so I work near it, humming. Mind its boiling, though; that comes in its own hour.",
      bloodreaver: "A pot hauled into the middle of the room, solid as a wall. Good shelter when he roars, and something to ram his face into later.",
      runeguard: "He leaps to the centre with the stockpot, and there it stays: shelter from his roar, a place to aim his charge, and a source of rings to read. It is the first fixed word on a page of moving ones.",
      gorgemaw: "At last a centrepiece: a stockpot dragged to the middle of the table, solid enough to shelter behind and something to drive him into. The broth smelled of nothing. Bland.",
      oathbroker: "A new fixture in the premises: the stockpot, placed in the middle for the remainder of the service. Solid, a shelter from the roar, a wall for the charge. One caution in small type: it boils over.",
      ironpalm: "He set the pot in the middle. It does not move. Something to stand behind. Something that boils.",
      ranger: "Pot in the middle. Solid. Cover from the roar, a post to aim the charge at. Watch its rings. Marked it on my map and drew a circle round it.",
      shadowblade: "A pot, dragged to the centre and abandoned there. Solid, wide, opaque. A lovely thing to stand behind. Its rings boil next, though.",
      pyromancer: "He dragged a giant copper pot into the middle of the floor, and it's solid! Something to hide behind when he roars. And it bubbles. I like it. I would like to marry it.",
      thermist: "Pot in the middle. Solid. Cover. Then the copper went hot and the rings began to boil off it. Watch them. I stood too close and my glass read nonsense.",
      driftblade: "The pot goes to the middle and stays there, which I can respect in a pot. Round it, behind it, and later through its rings, like a swift through a bell tower.",
      marionettist: "A new set piece, centre stage: the stockpot. Solid scenery, cover for the roar, a target for the charge. Rings boil off it later; do not lean. (The Doll, leaning on it, very casual.)",
      cleric: "After the fire he hauled the great pot to the middle of the kitchen and left it there. It shelters us from his roar, and our knight can lead his charge into it. The rings it gives off I like less.",
      grovekeeper: "The stockpot settled in the middle like a boulder after a landslide. Solid ground to shelter behind and a place to steer his charge. Rings boil off it after.",
      chronomancer: "Pot to the middle, a leap and a thud. It stays. Cover from the roar, a wall for the charge, and the rings come next, out then in. A fixed note in a restless piece.",
      alembist: "Note for the shelf: the stockpot, now in the middle. Uses: shelter from the roar, a wall for the charge. Hazard: it boils over in rings. Label both, and do not lick the rim.",
      threadweaver: "He planted that pot in the middle for good. Something to tie the hem behind when he roars, and our knight can lead his charge into it. Finally, a fixed point to sew to.",
      tidecaller: "He dragged the pot to the middle and left it, and a thing that stays put is worth a good deal. It turns his roar aside, and the knight can lead his charge into it. I took my stand close by and watched the rings it gives off.",
      lanternkeeper: "He dragged the pot to the middle and left it, and it turns his roar aside. I kept my lanterns in its shelter but not too close, since the pot gives off rings. A thing that stays put is a fine lamp-post to count from.",
      kenseier: "He dragged the pot to the middle and left it. It turns his charge and shelters us from his roar, so I fought close beside it. A fixed point is a fine thing for a man who counts.",
      houndmaster: "He dragged a pot to the middle and left it. Turns his charge, shelters us from the roar. Lads lay down behind it. Mind the rings round it, though.",
    } },
    'Simmer': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The pot boiled over in rings, out from the rim and then back in. I stood clear of each until it had boiled, then stepped in behind it. Step, wait, step. Easy ground, if you count.",
      gloomsworn: "The pot boiled in rings, going out and then returning. I waited just beyond a ring and moved in as it passed over. Arrive after the blow, as the dead do.",
      thresher: "Rings boiled out of the pot and back in again, like ripples from a stone in a pond. I waited outside each and stepped in as it passed. Quiet ground behind a wave, and a pretty rhythm to it.",
      bloodreaver: "Rings of boiling water off the pot, out and then back in. I waited, then followed each ring just after it boiled. Barely touched me. Shame.",
      runeguard: "The coils go dull, then red, then white, ring by ring: the colour is the clock. Step into the ring that has just boiled, outward on the first wave, inward on the second.",
      gorgemaw: "The pot boiled over, ring after ring, out and then in. I followed each ring once it had boiled. The broth was thin and the service slow.",
      oathbroker: "The rings come due in order, outward and then inward. A ring already paid owes nothing, so I stepped into each behind it. No receipt was issued for any wave, I regret.",
      ironpalm: "Rings left the pot, then returned. I waited at the edge of each and stepped in as it passed. Water is patient, until it is not.",
      ranger: "Rings out from the pot, rings back in. Stepped in behind each as it passed. Watch the colours.",
      shadowblade: "The pot boiled over in rings that went out and came back. I waited for each wave to pass and slipped in behind it, as I do. Patience is a shadow's best friend.",
      pyromancer: "The rings round the pot go dull, then red, then white, and I loved every colour. I followed each one after it boiled, out and then back in. Like a dance. A hot dance, and I led.",
      thermist: "Rings: dull, red, white. Wait for white. Step into it. The next one boils behind me and I'm already on to the next, out and back in. Every ring a degree wiser.",
      driftblade: "Rings boiled outward from the pot, and I went out behind each, through it as it finished, and when they came back in I went back through them the other way.",
      marionettist: "Ripples of boiling water from the pot, out then in, as choreographed as any waltz. The company steps into each ring just after it boils. (The Doll, dancing.)",
      cleric: "The pot's rings boil one after another, out and then back in. I led our people into each ring just after it boiled, and nobody was scalded.",
      grovekeeper: "Simmer comes like ripples in a pond after a stone, dear. Step into each ring just after it passes, out first, then in. My lot rode the ripples.",
      chronomancer: "Rings out, a beat apart, then back in. I step in behind each as it passes; the second wave is the first one backwards. I noticed that before it happened, which is cheating, but quietly.",
      alembist: "Rings of boiling water from the pot, outward, then inward. Step into each ring just after it boils. A pot simmering is how most of my mistakes begin.",
      threadweaver: "The rings boil out from the pot and back in. I kept my stitches walking just behind each ring as it boiled, no one ahead of the thread.",
      tidecaller: "The pot boiled in rings, out and then back in. I waited for each to pass over and stepped into the ground it had left, never ahead of the water. Follow the swell; do not go out to meet it.",
      lanternkeeper: "The pot boiled in rings, out and then back in. I stepped into the ground each ring had just left and carried the light in after me, never ahead of it. We were never standing where a ring was about to be.",
      kenseier: "The pot boiled in rings, out and then back in. I waited just outside each until it passed, then stepped in behind it. One beat to wait, one to go. He keeps good time, for a cook.",
      houndmaster: "Pot boils in rings, out and then back in. Stood just outside each till it passed, then stepped in behind. Held the dogs, who'd have run right into it.",
    } },
    'Whisk': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cross of beams through him that turned, a notch at a time. I kept to the gap between beams and walked round with it. Slow work, then quick.",
      gloomsworn: "Beams crossed in him, turning slowly one notch at a time. I found the open wedge and went round with it. Slow things suit me.",
      thresher: "He had a cross of beams through him, turning a notch at a time like the hand of a clock. I found the gap between two arms and strolled round with it. Never hurried, that one, and neither was I.",
      bloodreaver: "A cross of light swung round the room, a notch at a time. I walked with the gap and took no wound. Embarrassing.",
      runeguard: "Wire loops form a cross through him and turn by stages. Stand in the gap between its arms and walk with the turn. Pleasingly regular; I could have set my watch by it.",
      gorgemaw: "Air, whisked in a cross through the room, turning as he went. I walked round with the gap. All air, no substance, and then too much soup.",
      oathbroker: "A cross of beams that turns by stages, and a gap in it for the careful. I kept to the gap and moved with each turn.",
      ironpalm: "The cross turned. I turned with it. It did not touch me.",
      ranger: "Cross through it, turning. Stay in the gap. Walk with the turn, never against. Like tracking a hawk that circles the same pole.",
      shadowblade: "A cross of beams, turning slowly through the room. I stayed in the gap and walked round with it. Away from the mess, as ever.",
      pyromancer: "The Whisk! A cross of beams through him, turning step by step. I walked with the gap and never stopped casting. Beam after beam, and I kept to the gap like a dancer between spotlights.",
      thermist: "Cross through him, turning. Stand in the gap. Walk with it. Then the heat, cutting him all the way round, rushing the whole circle. Dizzy. Delighted.",
      driftblade: "A cross of beams turning through him, and I turned with the gap, round and round, a falcon on a thermal.",
      marionettist: "The revolving set: a cross of beams turns through the ogre, a notch at a time. The company walks with the gap. (The Doll, keeping step.)",
      cleric: "His whisk turns a cross of beams through the room, step by step. Walk round with the gap between them. Our archer walked the wrong way once, and I thanked the light the poor thing lived.",
      grovekeeper: "The Whisk turns like a weathervane in a steady wind, dear. Stand in the gap and walk with it. My lot kept pace.",
      chronomancer: "A cross through him, turning a notch each beat. Walk with the gap, on the beat. The spin was the same last time. It will be again.",
      alembist: "A cross of beams, turning in steps. Stand in the gap and go round with it, the way it turns. Write it on the label. Stir with, never against.",
      threadweaver: "A cross of beams turning through him, a notch at a time. I walked my stitches round with the gap like thread winding onto a bobbin.",
      tidecaller: "A cross of beams turned through him, a notch at a time. I found the open wedge between them and walked round with it, never against the turn. Slow work suits me.",
      lanternkeeper: "A cross of beams turned through the kitchen a notch at a time. I found the open wedge and walked round with it, trailing a lantern behind me.",
      kenseier: "Beams crossed inside him and turned one notch at a time. There was an open wedge between them, and I went round with it, step for step, never against it. Slow, and honest.",
      houndmaster: "Beams cross inside him and turn slow. Found the open wedge and walked round with it, never against, the dogs a step behind me. Like walking a dog round a post.",
    } },
    'Spice Rack': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Jars of spice poured on set spots, and one followed each of the others. They carried theirs clear of everyone and stepped off before it poured. I held the middle and kept my eyes on his back.",
      gloomsworn: "Spice rained on fixed spots, and a jar chased each of us about the floor. We drew ours away and slid aside before it poured.",
      thresher: "Jars of spice hung over certain spots, and one hung over me and followed along like a shy little cloud. I led mine away from everyone and stepped aside before it poured.",
      bloodreaver: "Spice jars poured all over the floor, and one over each of the soft ones. They moved off theirs before it poured. Spicy!",
      runeguard: "Jars shake over set places, and one over each of us not before him. Carry yours to clear floor and step off before it pours. The fixed spots I copied into the plan; the wandering jars I trusted to the others.",
      gorgemaw: "He seasoned the floor, and each guest besides. My friends led their jars away and stepped aside before they poured. Over-seasoned, and not a grain on the meat.",
      oathbroker: "A fixed pattern of pours, plus one account per co-signatory but the shield. Each carried theirs clear and stepped off before payment fell due.",
      ironpalm: "Jars followed the others across the floor. Each led theirs to empty ground and stepped aside before it poured. The jar fell on nothing.",
      ranger: "Jars over set spots, one over me. Moved mine clear of the others, stepped off.",
      shadowblade: "Spice jars shaken over the floor and one over me personally. I took mine somewhere empty and stepped aside before it poured.",
      pyromancer: "A jar over my own head, and more over the floor, as if I were a roast. I walked mine clear of the others and dodged before it poured. Sneezing, and thoroughly seasoned.",
      thermist: "Jar over me. Carry it clear. Off it before it pours.",
      driftblade: "A jar hung over me, so I carried it through the room away from everyone and slipped off before it poured.",
      marionettist: "Jars of spice over set marks, and one over each of the company. Each carries theirs offstage and exits before it pours. (The Doll, sneezing.)",
      cleric: "The spice jars pour on the same places each time, and one follows each of us but our knight. Carry yours away from everyone, step off before it pours.",
      grovekeeper: "Spice Rack sows on set rows, dear, and one seed follows each of my lot. Carry it to empty ground and step off before it drops.",
      chronomancer: "Jars on set spots, one following each of us; it stops following just before it pours. Step off then. I have danced this one.",
      alembist: "A jar over each of us but our shield, plus fixed spots on the floor. Carry yours to clear floor, step off before it pours. Note: a wasteful use of good spice, and I say that as someone who has set fire to cinnamon.",
      threadweaver: "A jar followed each of my stitches; I let them out apart so no two poured together.",
      tidecaller: "Jars poured on fixed places, and one followed each of us but the knight. We carried ours well apart and stepped off before it poured. I kept the water ready for both.",
      lanternkeeper: "A jar hung over every one of us but the knight, and others poured on the same spots as before. I walked mine away from the rest and moved off before it tipped.",
      kenseier: "Spice poured on fixed spots, and a jar chased each of the others. They drew theirs away from everyone and stepped aside before it fell.",
      houndmaster: "Jars on fixed spots and one hunting each of us. Drew mine away from the rest and stepped off at the last.",
    } },
    'Boil Over': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He boiled over, and the closer you stood, the worse it scalded. We all got as far from him as the room allowed, me included. A post is no use if you are cooked at it.",
      gloomsworn: "He boiled over; the closer, the worse the scald. We all went to the far side of the kitchen, me included. A post can be left briefly, when the post itself burns.",
      thresher: "He boiled over, and the nearer I stood the worse it scalded. So I strolled to the far wall, as far as the kitchen goes, and came back to him once it settled.",
      bloodreaver: "Boil Over! Closer means hotter. I wanted to stand next to him for it. I didn't. Ran to the far wall like everyone else, and still came away with a good scald.",
      runeguard: "Rolling heat and bubbles gather about him, and the harm falls away with distance. All of us to the walls, then, as far from him as the room allows. A short rule, and nobody argued with it.",
      gorgemaw: "As every bad cook eventually does, he boiled over. Closer was hotter, so even I backed off to the walls. A scalding broth, and no one to thank for it.",
      oathbroker: "The debt scales with proximity to the Chef. We all retreated to the walls to reduce the sum, myself included, and I do not mind admitting that I ran.",
      ironpalm: "The heat rose from him. Near, it shouts. Far, it whispers. I went far.",
      ranger: "Closer to it, worse it burns. Ran to the far wall. Kept shooting. Wind carries heat, but not that far.",
      shadowblade: "He boiled over, and the closer one stood, the worse it scalded. So I put the whole kitchen between us. Distance is its own kind of shadow.",
      pyromancer: "Boil Over! The closer you stand, the more it scalds. I'm always at the back anyway, so I went even further back. From the far wall it looked like a very bright soup.",
      thermist: "Heat, rising, too much; I ran hot and ran far. The closer you stand, the worse it gets. The far wall is the only answer. My glass agreed.",
      driftblade: "The nearer you were the worse it burned, so I ran, through and away to the far wall, faster than I have ever run, and a hawk would have called it graceful.",
      marionettist: "A great swell of steam from the star; the nearer the footlights, the worse it scalds. The company retreats to the back wall and watches the rest from there, the Doll steamed pink.",
      cleric: "When the heat rolls off him, the nearer you stand, the worse it is. Everyone, even our knight, ran to the walls, and I mended them before it came. A retreat is no shame.",
      grovekeeper: "Boil Over is a heatwave from the hearth outward, dear. Get as far from it as you can. My lot ran to the walls, and I planted on them before it broke.",
      chronomancer: "Heat swells off him, a long beat, then it boils over. Every step away is a step less burnt. We were all at the walls by the end of the beat.",
      alembist: "Scalding heat, worse the nearer you stand. Distance, all of us, to the walls, and a dose ready before. Note: boiling over is the worst thing a pot can do. I know. I have been the pot.",
      threadweaver: "The nearer to him, the worse the scald, so I let every thread out to the walls, our knight's too, and pulled them back once it had boiled. Even a good seam needs room.",
      tidecaller: "The nearer to him, the worse the scald, so we all went to the far walls, the knight too. I raised the water as we ran, so it was high when the heat broke. The edge of a wave is gentler than its heart.",
      lanternkeeper: "The nearer to him, the worse the scald, so we went to the far walls, the knight too. I set the lanterns on the far side before he began, so there was a light to run to. The edge of it is gentle, if you are early.",
      kenseier: "He boiled over, and the nearer you stood, the worse it scalded. All of us went to the far side of the room, I included. No blade turns a scald. Better to be far, and come back to him cool.",
      houndmaster: "He boils over, and the nearer you stand the worse it scalds. Made for the far wall, dogs ahead of me. Back to him once it cools.",
    } },
    'Last Orders': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the trolley came silence, then Last Orders on the whole room. No step escapes it. We gathered by the healer, mended in the quiet, and braced shoulder to shoulder.",
      gloomsworn: "The trolley, a long hush, then Last Orders on all of us. Nowhere to step. We pressed round the healer in the hush and were mended ahead of it. I make good use of silence.",
      thresher: "A hush fell after the trolley, the kind that comes just before the music starts. We gathered round our healer in it and got mended, so Last Orders found us ready.",
      bloodreaver: "Last Orders: a scorcher across everyone. We came in close, got mended in the quiet before it, and took it together. Gorgeous, and the best one yet.",
      runeguard: "A hush follows the trolley; then he calls it. Use the silence to gather and mend before the blast that reaches all of us. Silence, in his kitchen, is a punctuation mark, and I have learned to read it.",
      gorgemaw: "After the trolley the cook paused, then called Last Orders and threw the last of his kitchen at everyone. We gathered, mended, and swallowed it together. A filling course.",
      oathbroker: "A final bill, payable by all, presented after a period of grace. We used that grace to restore our means, then settled together, to the last coin.",
      ironpalm: "Silence. Then he called to everyone at once. We had gathered in the silence. We answered.",
      ranger: "Quiet after the trolley. Then the big one hits everyone. Heal in the quiet. Take it together. Hate waiting. Worth it.",
      shadowblade: "After the trolley, a lull, and then the mark called Last Orders and hit everyone at once. I spent the lull being mended. Even shadows need a little care.",
      pyromancer: "The quiet after the trolley was eerie, and then Last Orders hit the whole room at once! We were together, and mended first. Not a bad bill, as bills go.",
      thermist: "Cold in the quiet: gather, mend, wait. Then the blast came hot and loud across all of us, and we held. Steady, for once, from first degree to last.",
      driftblade: "A hush after the trolley, and I had to stand still with everyone, ugh, and then the whole room was hit and we took it together, and I could finally breathe.",
      marionettist: "A pregnant pause after the trolley, then his final call to the whole house. The company gathers and is restored during the pause, and holds its breath on cue. (The Doll, bracing dramatically.)",
      cleric: "After the trolley there was a hush, and I used every moment of it. When Last Orders came, everyone was close and whole, and we came through it.",
      grovekeeper: "Last Orders is the first frost, dear: you know it's coming, and you have the quiet before it. I gathered my lot and planted on them all in that quiet.",
      chronomancer: "Silence after the trolley; the blast comes after it, every time. We gathered in the silence and were whole by the bell. I was ready. I am nearly always ready after the fact.",
      alembist: "One great blast at everyone, after a long silence. Use the silence: everyone mended and gathered. Note: the shelf should still hold something by then. Mine held two vials and a biscuit.",
      threadweaver: "In the quiet after the trolley I drew everyone in close and mended every stitch, right to the selvedge. Then Last Orders hit us all, and the seam held.",
      tidecaller: "After the trolley came a long hush, and I spent every breath of it. We gathered close and I filled the water to the brim, so when the great blow fell on all of us it broke on a full shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "After the trolley came a long hush, and I spent every breath of it. We stood close, every wick brought back to gold, and when the great blow fell on all of us I let every lantern burst at once. Three glows over four of us.",
      kenseier: "A trolley, a long hush, then Last Orders on the whole room. Nowhere to step and nothing to cross. We gathered by the healer in the quiet and were mended before it fell. I have rarely valued a hush more.",
      houndmaster: "Trolley rolls in, long hush, then the whole room gets it. Gathered by the healer in the quiet, dogs too, and got mended first. Nothing to dodge. The hush is your warning.",
    } },
    'Gorge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "At the end he just ate, and every mouthful hit all of us, harder each time. Nothing to hold but the line. We had to put him down fast. Held, long enough.",
      gloomsworn: "In the end he only ate, and each mouthful struck every one of us harder than the last. Nothing to hide behind, so we killed him fast. The dark kept me upright to the end.",
      thresher: "At the end Grubnak only ate, and every bite came in on all of us, heavier each time. Nothing to dodge, so I put everything into the cut and brought him in before he finished his supper. A hungry man, and an appointment kept.",
      bloodreaver: "Gorging, every bite hitting us all, harder each time! Wonderful. But you cannot outlast it, so we hit him with everything until he fell.",
      runeguard: "He eats. Each bite reaches us all and grows heavier; it cannot be outlasted, only ended. Strike with everything that remains. The last page is always shorter than one hopes.",
      gorgemaw: "He began to eat his own cooking, and every bite hurt his guests more. A rival at the table! I made very sure he did not finish his meal.",
      oathbroker: "A compounding debt, each instalment heavier than the last, owed by all. It cannot be serviced for long; only foreclosing on the Chef settles it. We foreclosed.",
      ironpalm: "He ate. Each bite louder than the last. We could not wait for him to stop. So we ended the conversation.",
      ranger: "Every mouthful hits everyone, each one worse. Shoot faster. Held my breath between draws, which helped nobody.",
      shadowblade: "At the end the mark simply ate, and every bite hurt everyone more than the last. No shadow helps with that. One just finishes the job quickly.",
      pyromancer: "At the end he just ate and ate, and every bite hit all of us harder! No dodging, only burning, so I burned brighter than I ever have. Dessert, I decided, was mine.",
      thermist: "Each bite hotter than the last, and it does not stop. Everything I have, now. My glass cracked on the last bite, which seems fair.",
      driftblade: "He just ate, and every bite hit us harder, and there was nowhere to run through to. So I spent every ounce of speed I had on him.",
      marionettist: "The finale: the ogre devours the scenery, and every bite hurts the whole company more. The only way out is to bring the curtain down quickly. (The Doll, striking with feeling.)",
      cleric: "At the end he ate, and every bite hurt all of us more. There is no enduring it for long; I kept them standing while the others struck him down. He was only hungry. We all end that way.",
      grovekeeper: "Gorge is the long winter, dear: each bite colder. No crop outlasts it, so I kept my lot rooted while they cut him down quickly.",
      chronomancer: "Each bite on the beat, harder each time. No undoing that for ever. So the others hurried, and I kept time.",
      alembist: "Every bite he takes hurts all of us more, and there is no remedy, not for long. Empty the shelves and end him. I threw everything I owned, including the vial I was saving for my birthday.",
      threadweaver: "He ate, and every bite tugged at every stitch, harder each time. A thread won't hold for ever, so we cut him down quickly, before anything frayed.",
      tidecaller: "At the end he only ate, and each mouthful struck all of us harder than the last. The water cannot stay high for ever against that, so the others struck him down fast while I held us up. He was only hungry. The sea knows that too.",
      lanternkeeper: "At the end he only ate, and each mouthful struck all of us harder than the last. The wicks burned low faster than I could trim them, so I topped them up and let them burst, and the others struck him down quickly. He was only hungry. I left one burning.",
      kenseier: "At the end he only ate, and every mouthful struck all of us, harder each time. No beat to catch, only a clock running. We put him down quickly. A fine cook, to the last mouthful.",
      houndmaster: "He only eats now, every mouthful worse than the last. No hiding, just killing him quick. Sent the dogs in with everything I had.",
    } },
    'Clogged Drains': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Grease pooled round the drains for good, and any fire lights it. I marked every drain on my map and kept the line off those patches. Bad footing is one thing; burning footing is another.",
      gloomsworn: "The drains backed up and the grease stayed round them. Fire will find it sooner or later, so I took the fight somewhere else and left that patch to the dark.",
      thresher: "On the harder nights the drains backed up, and grease pooled round them and stayed, dark and shiny. Any spark sets it burning, so I kept off that ground all evening and worked the drier floor.",
      bloodreaver: "The drains backed up and spat grease round them for good. It burns when fire finds it. I kept my boots off it. A fine scar is one thing; burning feet are another.",
      runeguard: "A gurgle from the drains, and the grease pools round each and stays; when fire comes, it burns. Mark the drains, keep off their grease. I added each to the floor plan in red.",
      gorgemaw: "On the harder nights the drains back up, pooling grease round them for good. A filthy kitchen. Harmless until fire reaches it, then I keep well clear. Disgusting.",
      oathbroker: "A permanent lien of grease round each drain, which comes due when fire reaches it. I noted their positions, and we stay clear of the property.",
      ironpalm: "The drains backed up. The grease stayed. When the fire came, it spoke through the grease. We stood elsewhere.",
      ranger: "Drains back up. Grease round each, for good. Fire lights it. Keep off it. Mapped them.",
      shadowblade: "On the harder nights the drains overflow with grease, and it stays. Any fire sets it alight. I keep well away from the drains, like a cat avoiding puddles.",
      pyromancer: "Grease round the drains for good, and grease plus fire makes a very bad evening, so I keep away from the drains. His stove, on the other hand, is a disgrace.",
      thermist: "Grease round the drains, for good. Harmless while cold. The moment fire reaches it, it burns. Off it before then. A stable system waiting for its spark.",
      driftblade: "The drains spill grease that stays, so I run round them now rather than through, which goes against my nature and my boots.",
      marionettist: "The drains overflow with grease, a permanent stain on the stage. Fire lights it, so the company keeps its marks elsewhere. (The Doll, wrinkling its painted nose.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights the drains overflow and grease pools round them for good. When any fire reaches it, it burns. I kept everyone off the grease by the drains.",
      grovekeeper: "The drains flood with grease like a field after a storm, dear, and it stays. When the fire comes, it burns. My lot keep their roots off that ground.",
      chronomancer: "The drains back up and the grease stays. It's harmless until the fire comes. Then it's not. I remember where the drains are the way one remembers a bad day.",
      alembist: "Grease round the drains, permanently. Harmless alone; with fire, it burns. Keep off it when fire is coming. Note: a reagent waiting for its partner. I have felt that.",
      threadweaver: "The drains overflowed and grease ringed them for good. Fire lights it. I kept my stitches away from the drains so nothing burned. Stains and fire both ruin good cloth.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights the drains backed up and grease lay round them for good, and fire always finds grease. I kept everyone off those patches. Slick shingle is bad footing; burning shingle is worse.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights the drains backed up and grease pooled round them for good, and fire always finds grease. I kept every lantern well clear of those patches, and the others with them. A lamp near spilled oil is a lesson a lamplighter learns once.",
      kenseier: "The drains backed up and the grease stayed round them for good. Any fire would light it, so I marked each drain and fought elsewhere. A patch that waits to burn is only a fuse, and I do not stand on fuses.",
      houndmaster: "Drains backed up and the fat stays round them for good. A spark and it goes up. Marked each one and walked the lads round them. Rust would have found every last one, silly lad.",
    } },
    'Belly Flop': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He flopped down where he stood, which is a thing he does on the harder nights. Out from under him, and open floor at my back, because the shock threw us. Then ladle out, lid in. A wall behind you is worse than the blow.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights he dropped flat on the spot. I moved out from beneath him with clear floor at my back, because the shock throws us, then read his back for ladle or lid. I know what falling feels like.",
      thresher: "On the hard nights he simply lay down on the spot, flat as a dropped tray. I stepped out from under with open floor behind me, since the shock shoves, and read his back: ladle, out; lid, in.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one flopped belly first, my friend! Out from under, then thrown back; open floor behind me, not a wall. Then the tool on his back said in or out. A fine fall.",
      runeguard: "His shadow swells beneath him, and the tool on his back names the sequel. Out from under, open floor behind to land on, then in or out as the tool decrees. I now check my back for walls before I check my front.",
      gorgemaw: "Belly first onto his own floor. Out from under him, with open floor behind me for when the shock threw me. Then ladle or lid. Overweight, both of us.",
      oathbroker: "Liability for those beneath him, and a throw for everyone. Take the throw onto open floor, not into a wall or a cask. Then the tool on his back sets the next terms.",
      ironpalm: "He fell. I moved out from under. The shock pushed me back, and I let it, toward open floor. Then the ladle spoke.",
      ranger: "Flops where it stands. Out from under. Thrown back, so open floor behind. Then the tool on its back: ladle out, lid in.",
      shadowblade: "He flopped down on his belly, terribly undignified. Out from under, thrown backward, so nothing hard behind me. Then the tool on his back tells me where to be.",
      pyromancer: "He belly-flopped, all of him at once! I ran out from under and let the shock fling me onto open floor, because a wall at my back would have been worse. Then ladle, out; lid, in. Pride: bruised.",
      thermist: "Out from under. Open floor behind me. Then the shock, a scalding fall, and the tool on his back says in or out. All the weight of him, delivered at once.",
      driftblade: "He flops where he stands. I went out through his shadow before it hit, and the shock threw me across open floor, a stone skipped on a pond, then in or out for the ladle or the lid.",
      marionettist: "A pratfall, which the house adored: out from under, the company thrown backward onto open stage, not into the scenery. Then the tool on his back cues the next scene. (The Doll, thrown, unbroken.)",
      cleric: "He flopped flat on the spot and the shock threw everyone back. Out from under first, with open floor behind, since walls and casks hurt, and then the ladle or the lid on his back. A hard fall, gently taken.",
      grovekeeper: "Belly Flop is an earthquake, dear. Out from under, and keep open ground behind for the shock to throw you onto. Then the tool on his back says in or out.",
      chronomancer: "He flops, a beat, then the shock throws us. Out from under first, open floor behind. Then the tool on his back on the next beat. I will have heard this one before.",
      alembist: "One ogre, flopping. Out from under, open floor behind for the throw. Then the tool on his back: ladle out, lid in. Note: casks behind you hurt. I know the exact bruise.",
      threadweaver: "He flopped and the shock threw my stitches apart, so I made sure each had open floor behind. Then in or out for the tool on his back. I will not have them landing in a wall like dropped buttons.",
      tidecaller: "He dropped flat where he stood and the shock threw us outward. I got out from under him with open floor at my back, for a wall or a cask hurts more than the blow, then read his back for the ladle or the lid.",
      lanternkeeper: "He fell flat where he stood and the floor kicked us outward. I made sure there was open floor behind me, since a wall or a cask hurts more than the blow, and walked my lantern clear of him. Then his back showed the ladle or the lid.",
      kenseier: "On the harder nights he dropped flat where he stood. I moved out from under him with clear floor behind me, since the shock throws a man, then read his back for ladle or lid. A wall at my back would have been worse than the blow.",
      houndmaster: "He flopped flat where he stood. Out from under him with open floor behind, since it throws you. Then read his back for ladle or lid. Don't leave a dog beneath him.",
    } },
    'Cleaver Toss': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He sent cleavers down lines at the two farthest from him. They turned the lines clear of us, stepped aside, and the steel came home the way it went. Stay off both ways. Thorough.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of us, farthest out, had cleavers sent along a line. Each turned theirs from the rest and stepped aside, and the blades came home by the way they went. I stayed off the line, out and back.",
      thresher: "He threw cleavers down lines at the two of us farthest off, and they come back up the same line. Each led their line clear of the rest of us and stepped off it, and I kept off both ways. A line is a line, coming or going, and easy to mind.",
      bloodreaver: "Cleavers thrown at the two furthest off, along lines! Off the line. Then they come back along the same line. I've had a cleaver in the back before; overrated.",
      runeguard: "Score marks run from him to the two farthest of us. Point the line away from the others, step off as it sharpens, and stay off: it returns the same way. The second reading is the one I nearly forgot.",
      gorgemaw: "The cook threw his cleavers at the two farthest guests and then called them back. We pointed the lines away from each other, stepped off, and waited. A tired dish.",
      oathbroker: "A debt delivered along a fixed line and returned along the same, and the return is where the Chef collects. I stepped off the line and stayed off it, and he collected nothing in either direction.",
      ironpalm: "He threw. It came along a line. I stepped off. It went back. I stayed off.",
      ranger: "Lines to the two farthest. Aim it clear of the others. Step off. It comes back the same way. Stay off.",
      shadowblade: "Cleavers thrown at the two of us farthest out. I aimed mine away from friends, stepped off, and waited; it came back the same way. Persistence is so tiresome.",
      pyromancer: "He threw a cleaver at me for being far away, which I always am! Point it away from the others, step off, and stay off; it flies back the same way.",
      thermist: "I'm farthest. Line from him to me. Aim it clear, step off. Hot: it flew past, then back, and I didn't move until it was home.",
      driftblade: "A cleaver line followed me because I'd drifted farthest out, so I pointed it away and slipped off it and stayed off while it went out and came back through.",
      marionettist: "Cleavers thrown along lines at the two players farthest upstage, then back along the same. Each points their line offstage, exits it, and stays out. (The Doll, ducking twice.)",
      cleric: "He throws cleavers along lines at the two farthest of us, and they fly back the same way. Point them away from everyone, step off, and stay off until they're home.",
      grovekeeper: "Cleaver Toss is a boomerang, dear. The two farthest point their lines away from my lot, step off, and keep off until it comes home.",
      chronomancer: "Cleaver out along its line, then back along it. The second pass is the one people forget. I remembered, having forgotten it already.",
      alembist: "Two cleavers, two lines, out and back. Point yours away from the others, step off, wait for it to come home. Note: they return. Things always return, especially the ones I throw away.",
      threadweaver: "Two cleaver lines to my farthest stitches. I had them point their lines away from the hem, step off, and stay off till the cleavers flew home. Out and back, like a needle.",
      tidecaller: "Cleavers flew along lines at the two of us farthest out, and came home the way they went. Each turned the line away from the others, stepped off it, and stayed off until the steel was back. A rip runs out and in on the same path.",
      lanternkeeper: "Cleavers flew down lines at the two of us farthest out, and came back along the same lines. I was not one of the pair, so I kept my glow away from those lines while they aimed them clear of everyone and stayed off until the steel came home.",
      kenseier: "A cleaver was thrown at each of the two farthest out, and it came back the way it left. They turned their lines from the rest and stepped off at the last, and I kept out of the lane both ways. A fine bit of throwing.",
      houndmaster: "Cleavers thrown at the two furthest out, and they come back the way they went. Turned mine away from the rest and stepped off at the last. Called the dogs off the lane both ways.",
    } },
    'Skewer': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A line ran out from him through me and followed me to the last breath before he threw. I took the skewer on my shield, or stepped off the line as it set, and the others kept clear behind me. The line runs where I stand, so I stand where it hurts nobody.",
      gloomsworn: "He hurled a skewer down a line that ran through me and kept following me until it set. I took it, the way I take everything, and kept my back to the empty wall so the line ran clear of the living. The rest stayed off the line behind me.",
      thresher: "A long red line ran out of him through our knight and followed him round until the very last, and then the skewer flew down it. The knight takes it, or steps off at the last, and none of us stands behind him on that line. Where he puts his feet, the line goes.",
      bloodreaver: "Skewer! He threw it down a line right through me, and I stood in it and let it find me, as one should. The others kept off the line behind me, and I turned my body so it flew toward bare wall. A little one, a nice change from the big ones.",
      runeguard: "The omen is a line from him through the shield-bearer, following until it sets, and then the skewer flies along it. I turn my stance so the line runs toward empty floor, and I take it or step aside at the last. The others keep off the line behind me.",
      gorgemaw: "He threw a skewer along a line that ran through me and trailed me until it set. I took it gladly, since skewers are only a snack, and the others kept off the line behind me. I stood so it flew toward the bare wall, not toward the guests.",
      oathbroker: "A line is drawn from him through my seat and follows it until a breath before delivery. The principal accepts the skewer, or steps off the line at the last; anyone standing behind me takes it as an extra, with interest. I sit where the line falls on nobody.",
      ironpalm: "The line ran out through me and followed until it set. I stood where it ran toward the wall, not toward my friends, and took the skewer on my palms. When I could, I stepped off at the last moment, and it flew past me.",
      ranger: "Line from him through the knight, trailing until it set. Skewer follows. Stay off the line behind the knight, and the knight puts it on bare wall. Took it, or stepped off at the last. Nothing behind him.",
      shadowblade: "A line ran out from him through our knight, and I kept well to the side of it. The skewer goes where the knight points it, so I watch where he stands and never behind him. Nowhere near it, naturally.",
      pyromancer: "A red line from his hand straight through our knight, trailing him until it locked, and then a skewer flew down it, like a very slow arrow! I stood well off the line, behind nothing at all. The knight turned it toward the wall; that is manners.",
      thermist: "Line from him through the shield, trailing, then locked. The skewer flies down it. Off the line, behind him, nowhere on the other side of him. He steps aside at the last, or takes it.",
      driftblade: "The line slid after our shield and then set, and the skewer flew down it, and I was nowhere near, which is rarer than it sounds. I swung wide of the line and never behind him. He took it, or slipped off the line at the end.",
      marionettist: "A spotlight of red ran from him through our leading player and trailed him until it set; then the skewer flew down the beam. The company stays out of the beam behind him. The knight takes his bow, or steps out of the light at the last. (The Doll, well to one side.)",
      cleric: "A line ran out from him through our knight and followed until it set, and then the skewer flew down it. I never stood behind him on that line, and I kept my light on the knight. He took it, or stepped off the line at the last.",
      grovekeeper: "A long line, dear, from him through our shield and trailing until it sets, then the skewer flies down it like a seed on the wind. My lot keep off the line behind the knight. He takes it, or steps aside at the last. I plant my mending on him before it lands.",
      chronomancer: "The line goes out through the knight and trails, sets, and the skewer follows a beat behind. Nobody stands behind him on the line. He takes it or steps off at the last. I had mended him already, and the line fell on bare floor.",
      alembist: "One skewer, down a line that runs through our patient and trails until it sets. Nobody stands behind him on the line, and he turns it toward the bare wall. He steps off at the last, or takes the dose. I stood off to the side.",
      threadweaver: "The line ran from him through our knight, trailing until it set. I kept my stitches off the line behind him, all of them, and the knight took the skewer or stepped aside at the last. A thread across that line gets cut.",
      tidecaller: "The line ran out through our knight, trailing him until it set, and the skewer flew down it like a spear down a channel. Nobody of ours stood behind him in the channel. He took it, or stepped off at the last, and the water stayed high round him.",
      lanternkeeper: "A line ran out from him through our knight and followed until it set, and then the skewer flew along it. I kept my lantern well off the line behind him, and so did the others. He takes it, or steps aside at the last.",
      kenseier: "A line ran out through me and trailed until it set. I stood so it flew toward the bare wall, and the others kept off the line behind me. Met it with the blade, or stepped aside at the last. Breathe, then move.",
      houndmaster: "A line from him through the shield-man, trailing till it set, then a skewer down it. Nobody on that line behind him, dogs included. He took it, or stepped off at the last. Rust stayed to one side, good lad.",
    } },
    'Mise en Place': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Three tools laid on the pass, left to right, the cleaver toward one side of its board. I put the order and the side in my book. Nothing hit us. It always comes later; that is what laying out means.",
      gloomsworn: "Three tools were set on the pass, left to right, the cleaver nearer one end of its board. I recited the order and the side to myself. All of it returns. I keep things.",
      thresher: "Three tools laid out in a line along the pass, left to right, the cleaver sitting nearer one end of its board. Nothing hurt yet, so I tucked the order and the side away to keep, the way one pockets a dance card.",
      bloodreaver: "Tools laid out like he was planning something: cleaver, ladle, lid, in some order, and the cleaver lying to one side. Remember it. He serves them later.",
      runeguard: "Three tools laid upon the pass, left to right, the cleaver resting toward one side of its board. Record the order and the side; they return later in that sequence. This is the table of contents.",
      gorgemaw: "Tools set out on the pass, left to right, as if preparing a banquet, the cleaver lying to one side. I committed the order to memory. Pretentious, but it is the one menu I trust.",
      oathbroker: "The Chef lays out the terms of a later service, left to right, the cleaver lying to one side. I read every clause, and then, for safety, read them again.",
      ironpalm: "He laid out three tools, left to right. I listened. He would speak them later.",
      ranger: "Three tools on the pass, read left to right. Cleaver lies to one side; marked which. They come back.",
      shadowblade: "Tools laid out very carefully on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side. I watched, as I watch everything, and remembered.",
      pyromancer: "He arranged his tools on the pass like a display! Left to right, cleaver to one side. Memorise it; it comes back much later. I made up a little song to remember it by.",
      thermist: "Cleaver, ladle, lid, left to right, in some order. The cleaver's side. Remember. It all comes back much later. Cool head, cool ledger.",
      driftblade: "Three tools on the pass and nothing else happening, which bored me, but I read them anyway, left to right, and which side the cleaver lay, and kept the lot in my head while I ran.",
      marionettist: "Props set out on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side: the script for a later scene. The company learns its lines. My Doll memorised hers in the wings.",
      cleric: "Three tools laid out on the pass, and the cleaver set to one side of its board. I tried to hold the lot in my head, and later he served them in that order. An old man's memory is a candle; I shielded it with both hands.",
      grovekeeper: "Mise en Place is the sowing, dear; the harvest comes later. Read the tools left to right and the side of the cleaver, and keep it in mind.",
      chronomancer: "Three tools, left to right, and the cleaver's side. I will need that later; I needed it last time.",
      alembist: "Specimens on the pass: three tools, laid left to right, the cleaver to one side. Serving order, I suspect. Labelled on my wrist, since the shelf was elsewhere.",
      threadweaver: "Three tools laid on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side. I stitched the order into my memory, in that order, and bit the thread.",
      tidecaller: "Three tools were laid on the pass, left to right, and the cleaver lay toward one side of its board. I kept the order and the side the way one keeps a tide table; the serving comes much later.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three tools were laid on the pass, left to right, and the cleaver lay toward one side of its board. I counted them off like lamps along a street: first, second, third, and which side. The serving comes much later.",
      kenseier: "Three tools laid on the pass, left to right, the cleaver nearer one end of its board. I said the order and the side to myself like a form. Nothing hit us. What is laid out is struck later.",
      houndmaster: "Three tools laid out on the pass. Learnt the order and which side the cleaver lay, and said it over to the dogs, since they never argue. He uses them later, in that order.",
    } },
    'Service!': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The tools came off the pass in the order he had laid them. The cleaver chops its side, the ladle means out, the lid means in, one after another. A drill I had been handed in advance.",
      gloomsworn: "The order on the pass was the order we lived. Cleaver for its half of the floor, ladle for out, lid for in. I called each a breath ahead so nobody had to guess.",
      thresher: "Cleaver, ladle, lid: he served them in the order he had laid them out, and I took them one by one. Cleaver for its half, ladle to get out, lid to get in. Not once late, which pleased me.",
      bloodreaver: "Service! In the order he had laid them: cleaver's side chopped, ladle sent us out, lid drew us in. Good rhythm, my friend. I enjoyed it.",
      runeguard: "The sign was given long before: the order on the pass. The cleaver chops its side, the ladle sends us out, the lid draws us in, each in turn. I had it written in the margin before he began, and I was pleased to find it correct.",
      gorgemaw: "He served his laid-out tools in their order: the cleaver's side chopped, the ladle outward, the lid inward. A tasting menu. Sent most of it back.",
      oathbroker: "The terms laid out earlier come due in order: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle without, lid within. I met each in turn, and initialled every margin.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke the tools in their order. Cleaver, ladle, lid. We answered each.",
      ranger: "Tools in order from the pass. Cleaver's side chopped. Ladle, out. Lid, in. Moved in sequence.",
      shadowblade: "Service! and the tools came out in their order: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in. I moved through them like a dance I had rehearsed, and he never once looked at the floor.",
      pyromancer: "The tools came out in the order he had laid them, and I barely had time to cast between: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. Rushed, but on time.",
      thermist: "The order, remembered. Hot: cleaver, cross; ladle, out; lid, in, one after another, burning through it.",
      driftblade: "The tools came off the pass in the order he laid them, and I flew the course: through the cleaver's half, out for the ladle, in for the lid, one after another.",
      marionettist: "The scene written earlier on the pass now plays. The cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in. The company hits every cue. (The Doll, flawless.)",
      cleric: "Service! The tools came out one by one in the order he had laid them: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. We moved together and I counted aloud for the slow of heart.",
      grovekeeper: "Service! is the harvest of what he laid out, dear. The tools come in the order we read: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. My lot reaped it in turn.",
      chronomancer: "Service! The tools come in the order they were laid, a beat apart: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. I had heard the melody long before he wrote it.",
      alembist: "Three tools in the order laid out. The cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in, in turn. Note: the label from earlier paid off. I may frame it.",
      threadweaver: "The tools came out in order, and I moved my stitches with each: the cleaver's side cleared, out for the ladle, in for the lid. Another neat seam.",
      tidecaller: "The tools came off the pass in the order he had laid them: the cleaver took its side, the ladle sent us out, the lid brought us in. I had the table by heart, so we were already moving when each one came.",
      lanternkeeper: "He served the tools in the order he had laid them: the cleaver's side first, then the ladle to send us out, then the lid to bring us in. I had walked that row in my head, as I would a street of lamps, and each step already had its light.",
      kenseier: "The tools came off the pass in the order he had laid them: cleaver for its half, ladle for out, lid for in. I called each a breath ahead of him. A fight handed over as a song, and I only had to keep time.",
      houndmaster: "Tools come off the pass in the order he laid them: cleaver's half, ladle out, lid in. Moved myself and the dogs a beat ahead of each. Easy as feeding time.",
    } },
    'Dinner Rush': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Nearest plate first. I held his east flank while the west was chopped, then crossed to his west inside the lid's quiet. Last, two of us stood together on one platter out past the ladle. Plain nights, the plates were already laid; hard nights, we set them where we stood.",
      gloomsworn: "The rush came in three beats: his east side for the first plate, the west edge of the quiet for the second. The third was a platter for two, far past the ladle. When the plates hung over our heads we set them ourselves. I have been served worse.",
      thresher: "A quick dance card, dear, nearest partner first: his east flank, a stroll round to his west where the lid's quiet lies, then a grand platter far out past the ladle, two of us waltzing onto it together. On the worst nights the numbers overhead decide who goes where.",
      bloodreaver: "Plates up! I took the close one on his east as the cleaver chopped the west, hopped across to his west for the second, and the last was a big platter out beyond the ladle for two. On the worst nights, the number over my head said which plate was mine.",
      runeguard: "The order follows distance. A plate at his east flank, a plate at his west where the lid's quiet thins, and at the last a platter beyond the ladle that wants two upon it. On the hard nights we draw the order ourselves, and a plate laid too near comes up too soon.",
      gorgemaw: "A dinner rush, one dish after another. First plate at his east elbow, second at his west inside the lid's quiet, and the finale a platter far past the ladle, shared by two guests. On hard nights we laid the table ourselves. Bland, but it kept coming.",
      oathbroker: "Each plate falls due in order of distance: his east flank, his west at the lid's quiet edge, then a platter beyond the ladle, owed by two signatories jointly. On the worst nights each carries a number overhead, and a plate must be taken by its own number.",
      ironpalm: "Three beats. East of him, I answered the first plate. West, inside the lid's quiet, the second. The third was a platter, far out, and two of us stood on it as one. When the plates were ours to lay, we laid them gently.",
      ranger: "Three plates, nearest first. East flank, then west just inside the lid's ring, then out past the ladle to the platter, two to a plate. Easy nights the plates are laid. Hard ones we set our own where we stand. Worst, your number is your plate.",
      shadowblade: "Served nearest first, an order I admire. East flank, close enough to hear him breathe; his west, in the lid's quiet; then a platter far from him, shared with a friend. On the worst nights a number over our heads told each of us where to be.",
      pyromancer: "Hot plates! The nearest comes up first, so I started at his east flank, then his west inside the lid's quiet, and finished on a huge platter far out past the ladle, sharing its glow with a fellow caster. On hard nights we set them ourselves; the worst added numbers.",
      thermist: "Plates, nearest first. Clipped: east of him, west at the lid's quiet edge, then a platter well out, two together. Hot and quick out there. We placed our own on hard nights. The worst added a number over each head. Match it to the plate.",
      driftblade: "Nearest plate first, so I went round his east flank as the west got chopped, slid through to his west and tucked into the lid's quiet, then ran out past the ladle to the platter and stood on it, still, with a friend. On the worst nights a number says which plate.",
      marionettist: "Blocking, last scene: the star's east side for the first plate, his west at the lid's quiet edge for the second, and a great platter far upstage for the last, two players on it together. On the worst nights each player's number is the mark to hit.",
      cleric: "The plates came nearest first. East, toward the sunrise, for the first; the sunset side inside the lid's quiet for the second; and the third a platter, far out, with two of us side by side on it. On hard nights we set them down ourselves, so I walked everyone through it.",
      grovekeeper: "Dinner Rush is sowing in rows, dear: the nearest ripens first. East furrow, then west where the lid's quiet thins, then a broad bed far out past the ladle for two of my lot together. On hard nights we sow the rows ourselves.",
      chronomancer: "Three bars, nearest plate first: his east side while the cleaver takes the west, his west within the lid's quiet, then one platter far out for two together. On the worst nights the numbers overhead tell us who plays which bar. I already knew mine.",
      alembist: "Dosage by distance. First dose, his east side; second, his west on the lid's quiet edge; last, a double dose in one dish far out past the ladle, two patients sharing. Pre-measured on plain nights, self-measured on hard ones. On the worst, numbers match the labels.",
      threadweaver: "Plates pinned in order, nearest first. East side for the first, west inside the lid's quiet for the second, then a platter beyond the ladle that two of us stood on, side by side. On hard nights we pin our own, and I check every hem before it comes up.",
      tidecaller: "The plate nearest him goes first, as a tide fills the near shore first: his east shingle, then his west where the lid's quiet laps, and last a broad platter far out, two of us wading on it. On the worst nights a number over each head named our plate.",
      lanternkeeper: "Dishes served nearest first. I counted them off: the first at his east, the second just inside the lid's quiet on the west, the last a platter out past the ladle with two of us on it and one lantern between. On the worst nights a number hung over each head.",
      kenseier: "Nearest plate first. I took his east flank, then his west within the lid's quiet, and the last beat was one platter out past the ladle, two of us on it together. On hard nights we laid them ourselves. With numbers overhead, I knew which plate was mine.",
      houndmaster: "Served nearest first. Near plate at his flank, next one over in the lid's quiet to the west, and the last a big platter well out past the ladle, two of us on it, dogs clear. Hard nights we set our own. Worst nights, a number over each head says whose.",
    } },
    'Clean Plate': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He breathed in and the whole room went with it: casks, pot, grease, all of it. The drag stops short of him, so I kept my footing and let it, and the line closed on the healer for the swell after. Nothing left to stand behind.",
      gloomsworn: "He drew the room into his mouth, cask and pot and every stain of grease. It hauled us toward him, but not far enough to matter. Then he swelled and the blast reached all of us, so we crowded the healer first. The kitchen is bare now. I know the feeling.",
      thresher: "Oh, he breathed in the whole kitchen, dear, casks and pot and all, and we were drawn a few steps his way, no closer than polite. Then he swelled up like a bad pudding and it reached everyone, so we gathered by the healer first.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one inhaled the room, casks, pot, the lot, and hauled us toward him. A gentle tug for a man like me. Then he swelled and the blast hit all of us, so we crowded the healer's elbow beforehand. Bleed together. The kitchen is bare now, and he is bigger. Good.",
      runeguard: "His chest drew in, and the casks, the pot and the grease went into his mouth. The drag halts short of him; it can be borne. The swell after it reaches everyone, so we stood close to the healer beforehand. The room, from then on, is bare.",
      gorgemaw: "My host inhaled his whole kitchen, casks and pot and grease, which is a cook eating his own larder. We were dragged toward the table but not onto it. Then he swelled, and the course reached every seat at once, so we sat close to the healer. Bloated.",
      oathbroker: "A clause I had not read: he inhales the whole room, casks, pot and grease forfeit, and draws us toward him, though never to his person. What follows binds everyone at once, so we gathered at the healer beforehand. The cover is gone for good.",
      ironpalm: "He took the whole room in. Casks, pot, grease, and we were drawn toward him, not into him. I did not fight it. Then he swelled and spoke to all of us at once, so we gathered by the healer. The kitchen was bare after.",
      ranger: "Beast sucked in the whole room, casks and pot and grease. It drags you its way, stops short. Didn't fight it. Then it swelled and hit everyone, so we'd bunched on the healer first. No cover left, and it's bigger now. Mark that.",
      shadowblade: "The mark drew in the whole kitchen, casks and all, and tugged us along, which was rude. The drag stops before his body, so I let it. When he swelled it reached everyone, so I slipped in beside the healer. Fewer shadows now.",
      pyromancer: "He breathed in the entire kitchen, pot and casks and grease, and dragged us toward him! I burned on through it, because the drag stops well short. Then he swelled, and the blast came for all of us, so I stood by the healer. Nothing left to hide behind.",
      thermist: "Clipped: he inhaled, the room went into him, and we were drawn toward him but stopped short. Let it. Then he swelled, and the heat came for everyone, and I was already close to the healer. Cover gone.",
      driftblade: "He breathed in the whole kitchen and I let it carry me toward him, because it stops well short, and when he swelled I was already through the crowd and in beside the healer where everyone gathered for the blast. Casks gone, pot gone, nothing to run round.",
      marionettist: "The scene changes: the star inhales the set itself, casks, pot and grease, and drags the company toward him, though never onto him. Then he swells and the blast reaches every seat, so we gathered by the healer in the wings. A bare stage after. (The Doll, unmoved.)",
      cleric: "He drew in the whole room, casks and pot and grease, and pulled us toward him, though not close. I held my ground and the others held to me. When he swelled the blast reached everyone, so I laid the light on them first, together.",
      grovekeeper: "He drew the whole kitchen in like a gale in reverse, dear, casks and pot and all, and dragged us along, but not far. I kept my lot close and sowed before the swell, so the mending was growing when it broke. The room is bare ground now.",
      chronomancer: "He inhales; the room goes in; we are drawn his way and stopped short, every time. Then the swell, on the off-beat, reaching everyone. We had gathered by me on the intake and I had them mended before it, will have had, did. The cover will not come back.",
      alembist: "The whole room swallowed, casks and pot and grease, and the party dragged toward the mouth, not into it. Let it, gather near me, and be mended before the swell that follows reaches everyone. Shelves emptied, cover gone. Do not plan on it.",
      threadweaver: "He swallowed the room, casks and pot and grease, and tugged every stitch toward him. It stops short, so I held the hem together and drew it close around me before the swell. Mind the slack on the way in. The cover is gone, so nobody wanders.",
      tidecaller: "He drew breath and the room ran into him as water runs through a sluice, casks and pot and grease with it. We were dragged along, not far, and I went with it. Then the swell came for everyone, so I gathered us close and raised the water first.",
      lanternkeeper: "He breathed in the whole kitchen, casks, pot and grease, and drew us toward him, though not close. I walked in beside the others and hung my lights on us all before he swelled. One lantern for each of them. No cover left, so I kept us together.",
      kenseier: "He drew the room in, casks and pot and grease, and the pull took us a few steps toward him. There is no beat in a breath; the blade cannot turn it, so I let it carry me and we closed on the healer before the swell. Cover gone.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad breathed in the whole kitchen, casks and all, and dragged the lot of us his way, dogs too. Stops short of him. Whistled Rust and Ash in tight and got close to the healer before he swelled. Nothing left to stand behind.",
    } },
    'Spit Up': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two of the others were marked, a red ring on each, trailing them. I kept my post. They led it well off from everyone, stepped out as it locked, and each lump settled into a cask where it fell. Good cover, if placed right.",
      gloomsworn: "He retched, and two of the living got a red ring each that trailed them. They walked it away from the rest and stepped clear as it locked. Where the lump fell a cask stood after, which is worth knowing. I was spared the smell.",
      thresher: "Two of us were chosen, dear, a red ring following each until the last moment. They walked it somewhere quiet, away from the rest, and stepped out as it locked. Each lump landed and sat down as a cask, and that is useful later. In time, everyone!",
      bloodreaver: "He hacked up lumps at two of the others. Not me, shame. Each had a red ring tracking them, so they dragged it away from the rest and stepped out as it locked. The lump landed and turned into a cask. A better wall than most things he throws.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a retch, and a red ring fastened to each of two heroes, trailing them. The answer: lead it well away from the others and step out when it locks. Each lump settles as a cask where it landed, so lead it where a cask is wanted.",
      gorgemaw: "My host brought up a lump for two of my friends, each with a red ring that followed them to the plate. They carried it away from the table and stepped aside as it landed. Leftovers, and then a cask, the only thing of his I would keep. Bland.",
      oathbroker: "Two co-signatories were named, a red ring following each until the last moment. I led mine clear of the rest and stepped out when it locked. Each lump settles as a cask where it fell, so choose the ground with care.",
      ironpalm: "He retched. Two were asked, a red ring for each that followed. They led it away from everyone and stepped out when it stopped following. The lump landed, and sat down as a cask. We kept it in mind.",
      ranger: "Two marked, a ring each, tracking them. I took mine out to the far wall, away from the rest, and stepped off as it locked. Lump landed, turned into a cask. Cover where I put it.",
      shadowblade: "He retched at two of us, a red ring trailing each. Mine I walked to an empty corner, away from friends, and stepped aside as it locked. The lump landed and hardened into a cask, and I took the shadow behind it.",
      pyromancer: "Two of us got a red ring, mine included! It followed me, so I led it off to the empty edge, away from my friends, and stepped out just as it locked. Where it landed, a cask. A fine wall for later.",
      thermist: "Two of us marked, a red ring each, tracking. Cold: walk it away from the others, step out as it locks. Hot: it landed behind me and I was already clear, jittery, and the lump had turned into a cask. Cover, where I chose. Steadied.",
      driftblade: "He coughed up a lump and it was mine, a red ring trailing me, so I ran it round the edge of the room and away from everyone and slipped out of it at the last, and it landed on nothing and sat up as a cask. Through, and away.",
      marionettist: "Two of the company are called downstage, a red ring dogging each. They lead it into the wings, clear of the others, and step out as it locks. The lump lands and rises as a cask: a handsome prop. (Exit the Doll behind it, stage left.) A tolerable scene.",
      cleric: "Two of my friends were marked, a red ring following each. I told them to walk it away from the rest and step out as it locked, and they did. Each lump settled as a cask, which can shelter us later. I mended the one who was slow.",
      grovekeeper: "Two of my lot were marked, a red ring tracking each, like rain following a cart. They led it to bare ground, away from the others, and stepped out as it closed. Each lump took root as a cask where it fell. A seed placed well is half a harvest.",
      chronomancer: "A retch, then the ring on two of us, trailing, and the lump coming down once it locked. We led it away from the others and stepped out on the lock. Where it fell, a cask, which would matter soon. I knew; I know; I did.",
      alembist: "Two of us wear a red ring that follows until the last moment. Lead it clear of the others and step out as it locks. Each lump settles as a cask; shelve it where a cask will be wanted. No explosion. Disappointing.",
      threadweaver: "Two of my stitches got a red ring each, trailing. I told them to pull it out of the seam, away from everyone, and step off as it locked. A lump became a cask where it landed. A ring dragged over a friend is a sloppy seam.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us were marked, the red ring following like a rip. They drew it out to the empty shingle, away from the rest, and stepped aside as it locked, and each lump settled as a cask where it fell. A cask on that shore is a breakwater, and we would want one soon.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two of us were marked, a red ring following each. They walked theirs to a quiet corner, clear of the others, and stepped out as it locked. Each lump settled as a cask, a small dark place to shelter in later. Two rings, two casks.",
      kenseier: "He coughed at two of us, a red ring following each until the last beat. Neither blow was for me. They led it clear of the others and stepped out as it locked. Each lump landed and settled as a cask, which is a shield of its own. A courtesy.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad coughed up lumps at two of the others, a red ring following each. They walked it out clear of the rest and stepped off as it locked. Dogs stayed by me. Each lump turned into a cask, and a cask is a good thing to stand behind.",
    } },
    'Sour Burp': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He belched a wide sweep at one of the others, aimed true and trailing them. They carried it away from the rest and sidestepped out when it locked. The acid it left in pools along its path I marked, one by one, and kept off. Clean footing.",
      gloomsworn: "A belch, wide as a barn door, at one of the living. They took it off to the edge, away from the rest, and stepped aside as it locked. It left pools of acid down its path that sour whatever they touch. I kept out of them.",
      thresher: "Such a wide, rude burp, and it followed one of us until just before. Our chosen one walked it to an empty corner and stepped aside when it locked, and the rest of us stayed out of its way. Do mind the puddles it leaves; they linger, and they sting.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one belched a cone at one of the soft ones and left puddles of acid down the line of it. Not mine. They led it away from everyone and sidestepped on the lock. The acid is a nasty scar for anyone who wades in. Keep clear.",
      runeguard: "A swelling throat is the sign, and then a wide cone fixed on one hero, trailing them. Lead it to an empty edge, step out as it locks, and the others clear of its path. It leaves pools of acid, which linger; each is marked in red in my margin.",
      gorgemaw: "My host belched, a wide sour one, straight at a guest. Nobody has ever sent back a worse aftertaste. The guest led it off to the corner and stepped aside as it locked, and we all kept off the pools of acid it left behind. Sour, and left on the plate.",
      oathbroker: "The clause: a belch, aimed at one co-signatory, wide enough to bind whoever stands in line. The named party leads it away from the rest and steps out as it locks. A rider: pools of acid remain along it for some time. Stay off the property.",
      ironpalm: "He belched at one of us, wide and sour. The one chosen led it away from everyone and stepped aside when it locked. We kept clear of what it left behind: acid that lingers where it fell. I did not answer it with my feet.",
      ranger: "Cone, wide, on one of the others, tracking till the last. They took it out to an empty edge and stepped off as it locked. Acid along the line after, three pools, lasting. Well clear.",
      shadowblade: "He belched at one of us, wide as a doorway and not at all polite. The target took it to an empty corner and stepped aside on the lock; I stayed out of its path. It left pools of acid that linger, so I stayed out of those too.",
      pyromancer: "He burped at me! A wide sour cone, following me till the last moment, so I ran it to the empty edge, away from everyone, and stepped out as it locked. The acid it left is lasting, and not worth stepping in. Thoroughly insulted.",
      thermist: "Wide cone, one target, trailing. Lead it to the edge, step aside at the lock. Then the sour heat of it: pools of acid left along the path, lingering, and I swung wide of every one. Too close and you scald.",
      driftblade: "The burp went wide and followed one of us, and when it was not me I ran clear of its line, and when it was I took it round the edge and slipped out sideways as it locked, and the acid it left along the path I went round, never through.",
      marionettist: "A burp, centre stage, aimed at one of the company, wide and unforgivable. The chosen player leads it to the wings and steps out as it locks; the rest stay clear of its path. It leaves acid pools along it. Keep the blocking away from those. (The Doll took the other aisle.)",
      cleric: "He belched a wide sweep at one of us, and I called the others out of its path. The one chosen led it away and stepped aside as it locked. The acid it left lingers and burns, so I kept everyone off it.",
      grovekeeper: "He belched a wide, sour wind at one of my lot, trailing them. They led it to the bare edge and stepped aside as it locked. It left acid along its path like blight, and nothing grows in it, so we kept off. Walk round the blight, not through it.",
      chronomancer: "The burp followed one of us, and when the lock came, it went. We led it to an empty edge and stepped out on the lock. The acid it leaves along the line stays a while, so we stay off it, stayed off it.",
      alembist: "A wide sour cone on one of us, tracking till the last. The chosen leads it to an empty edge and steps out on the lock; everyone else stays off its line. It leaves acid on the floor that lingers, so label it and avoid it. Corrosive.",
      threadweaver: "He burped a wide cone down one stitch. The chosen one pulled it out of the hem to the empty edge and stepped aside on the lock. It left acid pools along the seam line, lasting, and I would not walk a thread through those.",
      tidecaller: "A sour swell, wide, aimed at one of us, following like a rip. The chosen led it out to the empty shore and stepped aside as it locked. It left pools of acid along its track that lingered like tide pools, and nobody waded in. The sea gives back; this does not.",
      lanternkeeper: "He belched a wide cone at one of us. I called the rest out of its path and the chosen led it to a dark corner and stepped out on the lock. It left acid in pools along the way, lingering. We walked round every pool.",
      kenseier: "A belch, wide, at one of us, with no beat to turn it. The chosen led it away from the rest and stepped aside on the lock. The acid it left along its path lingers, and a blade does nothing against it, so we walked round. Honest enough, for a cook.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad belched a wide one at one of the others, trailing them. They walked it to an empty edge and stepped aside on the lock; I called Rust and Ash well out of its way. Acid left in pools after, lasting. Not to be trod in.",
    } },
    'Second Helping': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He breathed in again, harder, and everyone was dragged toward the ring at his feet. A cask well out is the answer: stand behind it and the drag stops short, outside the ring. I was behind mine early.",
      gloomsworn: "He drew breath again, hard enough to drag us into the ring at his feet. A cask stops the drag short, so the living stood behind one well out, or took a far corner. I stood behind mine. I have been dragged by worse, but not into that.",
      thresher: "He took a second breath, dear, a hungry one, and everything was pulled toward the ring at his feet. A cask well out stops the pull short, so I stood behind one, or a far corner will do. Do mind the distance.",
      bloodreaver: "He took another gulp and dragged us toward the ring at his feet, then it went off like a stove. I stood behind a cask well out, so the drag stopped short of the blast. Missed a good scar, but the soft ones did too.",
      runeguard: "A second breath, harder than the first, and the red ring painted at his feet from the start. Be behind a cask, well out, so the drag ends short of the ring; a far corner serves too. Be there beforehand.",
      gorgemaw: "My host called for seconds and dragged every guest toward the ring at his feet, where the dish was being served. I sat behind a cask, well out, and the drag ended short of the plate. A far corner will do for the nervous.",
      oathbroker: "The second breath, and a clause for it: all are drawn toward the ring at his feet, where the sum falls due. A cask well out, with the party behind it, stops the draw short of the ring; a far corner has the same effect. Position taken beforehand.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed in again, and the room was drawn to the ring at his feet. I stood behind a cask, well out, and it stopped the pull short of him. A far corner would also have answered. I did not meet him in the ring.",
      ranger: "Second breath, harder. Everything dragged toward the ring at his feet, then it goes off. Cask between you and him, well out, stops it short. Or a far corner. Took the cask.",
      shadowblade: "He took a second gulp, greedy of him, and the whole room slid toward the ring at his feet. I stood behind a cask, well out, so there was a solid thing between us and the pull stopped short. A far corner works too.",
      pyromancer: "He gulped again, and everything slid toward the ring at his feet, where the blast waits! I planted myself behind a cask, well out, and the drag stopped short of the glow. A far corner will do, if you are quick.",
      thermist: "He inhaled again: ring at his feet, a drag toward it, then the blast. A cask between me and him, well out, stops the drag short. Far corner as well. Be there before the breath. I ran hot getting there, and steadied behind it.",
      driftblade: "He breathed in again and the whole room started sliding toward the ring at his feet, but I was already round behind a cask well out, and the drag stopped short of me, and I stood still, actually still, which I hate, for the whole of it.",
      marionettist: "The second breath, and the whole company is drawn toward the red ring at the leading man's feet. Take your mark behind a cask well out, or in a far corner, before the cue; the drag stops short of the ring. (The Doll, upstage, firmly behind a barrel.)",
      cleric: "He breathed in again, and everyone was dragged toward the ring at his feet. A cask well out stops the drag short, so I called them behind one, or to a far corner, before he breathed. We stood out of the ring, and I stood with them.",
      grovekeeper: "A second breath, like a gale in reverse, dragging us toward the ring at his feet. A cask well out is a good windbreak: the drag stops short behind it. A far corner serves. My lot were planted there before he breathed.",
      chronomancer: "The second breath: everything drags toward the ring, then the blast, hard on its heels. Behind a cask, well out, the drag stops short of it; a far corner too. We were there before he breathed, or will be, were. Not one of us needed undoing.",
      alembist: "A second inhale, with a drag toward the ring at his feet and a blast on arrival. Stand behind a cask well out, or in a far corner, before the first breath; the drag stops short. Nothing in my kit cures it after. A small fire, unrelated.",
      threadweaver: "Another breath, and every stitch tugged toward the ring at his feet. A cask well out stops the tug, and a far corner does the same. I tied us in behind the cask before he breathed, close and tight, with no loose end left to be dragged in first.",
      tidecaller: "The second breath came like a rip, dragging us all toward the ring at his feet. A cask well out is a breakwater; behind it the pull stops short, and a far corner is a quiet cove. We were behind it before the water turned.",
      lanternkeeper: "He breathed in again and drew everyone toward the ring at his feet. A cask well out stops the drag short, so I walked ahead and brought them behind it before he breathed. One cask, four of us, and the lamps low.",
      kenseier: "A second breath, a long one, and the drag toward the ring at his feet. The blade cannot turn a breath. A cask well out stops it short, and a far corner as well; I was behind one before the beat began. Honest of him to paint the ring first.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad gulped again and dragged everyone toward the ring at his feet, dogs too. Stood behind a cask well out and held Rust and Ash close; it stopped the drag short of the ring. A far corner will do. The shield-man found one too.",
    } },
    'Kitchen Staff': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two sous-chefs ran in from the larder doors with orders for the counter. I met one and stood by it, and it fell in behind me like a recruit. The rest struck the second down. Any that reached the counter put an extra plate on the next order.",
      gloomsworn: "Two cooks came running from the larder doors, bound for the counter. I went out and stood near one and it fell in behind me, and the others broke the second. A broken one drops a cask. A cook who gets through hands the next orders an extra plate, and someone must stand on it.",
      thresher: "Two little sous-chefs came dashing from the larder doors with orders for the counter, so punctual. Our knight stood close to one, which then trailed after him like a lamb, and the rest of us broke the other. Any that arrive put another plate on the next order.",
      bloodreaver: "Two cooks ran in from the larder doors! I stood near one and it followed me about like a pup, and my friends carved the other apart. A broken one drops a cask for us. One that reaches the counter is an extra plate for the next order. Good sport.",
      runeguard: "Two cooks emerge from the larder doors and make for the counter, each carrying an extra order. Stand close to one and it follows you, which keeps it from its goal; break the other. A broken cook leaves a cask, and each that arrives adds a plate to the next order.",
      gorgemaw: "Two sous-chefs scuttled in from the larder doors with more plates for the counter, as if my host had not served enough. I stood close to one and it trailed me about. The others broke the second. A broken cook leaves a cask, and each one served adds a plate.",
      oathbroker: "Two couriers entered by the larder doors, each bearing an additional order for the counter. By the clause, one stays with me if I stand close, and the others break the second. A broken courier leaves a cask in forfeit; each that reaches the counter adds a plate to the next order.",
      ironpalm: "Two cooks ran from the larder doors toward the counter. I stood near one and it followed me, and the others broke the second. A broken one leaves a cask behind. Any that reached the counter put one more plate on the next order. We did not let them pass.",
      ranger: "Two cooks from the larder doors, running for the counter. Knight walks up to one and it falls in behind him. Rest of us broke the other. Broken one drops a cask, cover for later. Anything reaching the counter adds a plate to the next order.",
      shadowblade: "Two sous-chefs scurried in from the larder doors carrying orders, and I took the second from behind, where cooks are most vulnerable. Our knight kept the first trailing after him. A broken cook leaves a cask; one that reaches the counter adds a plate to the next order.",
      pyromancer: "Two sous-chefs trotted in from the larder doors with more orders for the counter! I burned one down before it arrived, and the knight collected the other, which trailed him about like a duckling. A broken one leaves a cask. Any that arrive add a plate to the next order.",
      thermist: "Cooks at the larder doors, both running for the counter. Cold work for the knight: stand next to one, and it trails. Hot work for the rest: break the other fast. It drops a cask. Whatever reaches the counter becomes another plate on the next order.",
      driftblade: "Two cooks came running from the larder doors, and I cut through the nearest, round and through again, until it was down, while the knight stood by the other and led it about on a string. A broken cook leaves a cask. One that reaches the counter adds a plate.",
      marionettist: "Entrance, stage left and right: two sous-chefs from the larder doors, bound for the counter with orders. The knight takes one in tow; the company breaks the other. A broken cook leaves a cask, a handsome prop. A cook who reaches the counter adds a plate to the next order.",
      cleric: "Two cooks came running from the larder doors with orders for the counter. I asked our knight to stand by one, and it followed him patiently; the others broke the second. Every cook that arrives makes another plate. A broken one leaves a cask to shelter behind.",
      grovekeeper: "Cooks came in from the larder doors like seedlings through a hedge, bound for the counter. The knight kept one close and it followed him, and my lot broke the other. A broken one leaves a cask, good shelter. A cook that arrives grows another plate.",
      chronomancer: "Two cooks run from the larder doors; I have seen this scene before, and it always ends at the counter. Keep one close to the knight and it follows. Break the other, and it leaves a cask. A cook who arrives is another plate to serve, later.",
      alembist: "Cooks with orders, two of them, from the larder doors. Dose: the knight stands near one and it follows him; the others break the second. A broken cook leaves a cask, which is shelter. One that reaches the counter means another plate on the next order, and a patient to stand on it.",
      threadweaver: "Two cooks ran from the larder doors for the counter. The knight stood by one and it followed him like thread on a spool; we broke the other. A broken cook leaves a cask. One that gets to the counter puts another plate on the next order, and someone must serve it.",
      tidecaller: "Two cooks came in from the larder doors like streams to a sink, headed for the counter. The knight stood close to one, and it followed him as a skiff follows a boat; we broke the other, and it left a cask like a stone in the shallows. Each that came ashore meant another plate.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two cooks came running from the larder doors toward the counter. I hung my lantern on the knight, who stood by one so it trailed after him, and the others broke the second. A broken cook leaves a cask, a place to shelter. One that arrived added a plate to the next order.",
      kenseier: "Two cooks ran in from the larder doors for the counter. I stood near one and it followed me; the others broke the other, and it left a cask behind. The next order had a spare plate for every cook that got through, and that plate wants a hand.",
      houndmaster: "Two cooks ran in from the larder doors for the counter. Shield-man walked up to one and it trailed after him; the rest of us broke the other. Broken one leaves a cask to hide behind. Any that reached the counter put another plate on the next order. Dogs stayed clear.",
    } },
    'Flashover': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A small orange dot hung over one of the others, and a wide blast trailed them until it went off. Not mine. The marked one took it to clean floor, away from every puddle, and the rest of us kept clear of them. Grease it touches catches fire, and fire runs on.",
      gloomsworn: "Someone else got the orange dot this time, small over their head, a wide blast following. They carried it off to bare floor, away from the grease, and the rest of us kept out of its reach. Where it fell on grease, the fire ran along it. I stood well away.",
      thresher: "A small orange dot over one of us, dear, and a great round blast trailing them until the hour. The chosen one walked it to clean floor, away from the grease and from everyone else, because fire runs from one puddle to the next. The rest of us kept well away.",
      bloodreaver: "An orange dot over someone else's head, shame, and a huge blast trailing them! They ran it to clean floor, clear of the grease and the rest of us. Anyone caught in it takes half again, and every grease puddle it touches goes up. I kept my distance.",
      runeguard: "A small orange dot over one hero's head, a wide blast following them, going off at the last. Carry it to clean floor, clear of grease and of the party; every stain it touches will ignite, and fire runs along touching stains. The rest stand outside its reach.",
      gorgemaw: "A small orange dot over one guest's head, and a great round blast shadowing them. They carried it to clean floor away from the grease, since any grease it touches flames up, and fire runs on to the next puddle. The rest of us leaned well out of its reach.",
      oathbroker: "One co-signatory was marked with a small orange dot, and a wide blast follows them to its date. They must deliver it to clean floor, clear of the grease and of the others; any grease it reaches ignites, and fire runs between touching stains. Whoever stands inside pays half again.",
      ironpalm: "An orange dot over one head, and a wide blast following it. The one marked walked to clean floor, away from the grease and from us, and let it go there. We stood outside its reach. Fire runs from stain to stain, so none was left near it.",
      ranger: "Orange dot on one of the others, wide blast trailing them. They took it out to clean floor, clear of the grease and the party. Grease it touches goes up, and fire runs along puddles that touch. Anyone inside takes half again. Stayed well out.",
      shadowblade: "An orange dot over someone's head, and a wide blast shadowing them, not mine, how restful. The marked one led it away from the party onto clean floor, clear of any grease, which burns, and burns onward. I kept well beyond its edge.",
      pyromancer: "An orange dot over my head, mine! A wide blast trailing me until it went off. I ran it to clean floor, away from the party and every puddle of grease, because it lights whatever it touches and the fire runs along. When it is someone else's, I keep out of their glow.",
      thermist: "Orange dot, one head, a wide blast trailing. Cold: the marked one goes to clean floor, away from everyone and the grease. Hot: any grease it reaches flares, and the fire passes between stains that touch. The rest of us stepped out of its circle.",
      driftblade: "An orange dot over one of us and a wide blast following, so the marked one ran it round the edge of the room and out onto clean floor, away from everyone and every puddle, because grease it touches lights and the fire runs on. The rest of us gave it room.",
      marionettist: "A small orange spotlight over one player, and a wide blast following them downstage. The chosen one leads it to clean boards, clear of the company and of the grease; every puddle it touches catches, and the fire runs on. The company stays out of its reach. (The Doll, upstage.)",
      cleric: "One of us bore a small orange dot, and a wide blast followed them until it went off. I told them to carry it to clean floor, away from everyone and from the grease, which ignites and carries the fire from puddle to puddle. Anyone who stood in it took half as much again.",
      grovekeeper: "An orange dot over one of my lot, and a broad blast following like a brush fire, dear. They led it to bare ground away from the rest and from the grease, which lights and passes the fire along. The rest of us stayed well out of its reach.",
      chronomancer: "A dot of orange over one of us, a wide blast trailing, and it goes off on schedule, every time. The marked one carries it to clean floor, away from the party and the grease, which would light and pass the fire on. The rest of us stood out of reach, as we do.",
      alembist: "Label: orange dot over one head. Contents: a wide blast that follows them. The marked one carries it to clean floor, away from the party and any grease; grease it touches ignites, and fire runs along touching puddles. Everyone else stays clear of its edge. Deliberate fire only.",
      threadweaver: "One stitch got a small orange dot, and a wide blast ran after them. I told them to pull it out of the seam to clean floor, away from everyone and every puddle of grease, because fire runs along grease like a lit hem. The rest of us stayed beyond its edge.",
      tidecaller: "A wide blast followed one of us like a rip, an orange dot over the head, and the water in me went still. The marked one drew it far out to clean dry floor, away from the party and from any grease, which takes fire and carries it. We kept to the far shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "An orange dot hung over one of us, with a wide blast following them. I kept my lanterns with the party while the marked one carried it to a bare corner, well clear of the grease. Grease catches and passes the fire on. The rest of us stayed outside its edge.",
      kenseier: "An orange dot over one of the others, and a wide blast following them. There is no beat in a blast to turn aside, so they carried it to clean floor, clear of the grease and the party. Grease it touches catches, and the fire runs on. We stood out of reach.",
      houndmaster: "Orange dot over one of the others, and a wide blast trailing them. They walked it out to clean floor, clear of the grease and the rest of us. Grease catches, fire runs puddle to puddle. Kept Rust and Ash well outside its edge.",
    } },
  },
};
